/* AI brain Q&A pack 15: famous people */
(window.AIQA = window.AIQA || []).push(
  ["what is archimedes famous for | archimedes principle | what is the archimedes principle", "**Archimedes** (c. 287–212 BC) of Syracuse is famous for the principle of buoyancy: an object in water is pushed up by a force equal to the weight of the water it displaces. Legend says he shouted \"Eureka!\" in his bath when he figured it out. He also explained levers and estimated the value of pi."],
  ["who was euclid | tell me about euclid | euclid", "**Euclid** (around 300 BC) was a Greek mathematician who worked in Alexandria, Egypt. He's often called the \"father of geometry.\""],
  ["what is euclid famous for | what did euclid write | euclid elements", "**Euclid** is famous for *Elements*, a 13-book textbook of geometry and number theory. It was used to teach math for over 2,000 years, and the flat-surface geometry taught in school is still called Euclidean geometry."],
  ["who was hippocrates | tell me about hippocrates | hippocrates", "**Hippocrates** (c. 460–370 BC) was an ancient Greek physician known as the \"father of medicine.\" He taught that illnesses have natural causes rather than being punishments from the gods."],
  ["what is the hippocratic oath | what is hippocrates famous for", "**Hippocrates** is famous for the **Hippocratic Oath**, a promise traditionally linked to him in which doctors vow to treat patients ethically and \"do no harm.\" Versions of it are still recited by new doctors today."],
  ["who was ptolemy | tell me about ptolemy the astronomer", "**Claudius Ptolemy** (c. 100–170 AD) was a Greek-Egyptian astronomer and geographer in Alexandria. His book the *Almagest* described an Earth-centered universe that was widely accepted for about 1,400 years, until Copernicus."],
  ["who was hypatia | tell me about hypatia | hypatia of alexandria", "**Hypatia** (c. 360–415 AD) was a mathematician, astronomer and philosopher in Alexandria, Egypt. She is one of the first women in history known to have taught mathematics, and she led a respected school of philosophy."],
  ["who was al-khwarizmi | who was al khwarizmi | tell me about al khwarizmi", "**Muhammad ibn Musa al-Khwarizmi** (c. 780–850) was a Persian mathematician who worked at the House of Wisdom in Baghdad. His book on solving equations gave us the word **algebra**, and the word **algorithm** comes from his name."],
  ["who invented algebra | who is the father of algebra", "**Al-Khwarizmi**, a Persian scholar in 9th-century Baghdad, is often called the \"father of algebra.\" His book *al-Jabr* gave algebra its name, although earlier mathematicians in Babylon, Greece and India also solved equations."],
  ["who was ibn al-haytham | who was alhazen | tell me about ibn al haytham", "**Ibn al-Haytham** (c. 965–1040), also called Alhazen, was an Arab scientist from Basra who worked in Cairo. His *Book of Optics* correctly explained that we see because light enters our eyes, and he is often called a pioneer of the scientific method."],
  ["who was ibn sina | who was avicenna | tell me about avicenna", "**Ibn Sina** (980–1037), known in Europe as Avicenna, was a Persian physician and philosopher. His *Canon of Medicine* was used as a medical textbook in both the Islamic world and Europe for centuries."],
  ["what is copernicus famous for | what did copernicus discover | nicolaus copernicus", "**Nicolaus Copernicus** (1473–1543) was a Polish astronomer famous for proposing that the **Sun, not Earth, is at the center** of the solar system. His book was published in 1543, the year he died, and kicked off the Scientific Revolution."],
  ["who was tycho brahe | tell me about tycho brahe | tycho brahe", "**Tycho Brahe** (1546–1601) was a Danish astronomer who made the most accurate naked-eye measurements of the stars and planets ever recorded, before telescopes. His assistant Johannes Kepler used that data to work out how planets move."],
  ["who was johannes kepler | who was kepler | tell me about kepler", "**Johannes Kepler** (1571–1630) was a German astronomer and mathematician. He discovered that planets travel around the Sun in **ellipses** (stretched circles), not perfect circles."],
  ["what is kepler famous for | what are kepler's laws | keplers laws of planetary motion", "**Kepler** is famous for his **three laws of planetary motion**: planets move in ellipses with the Sun at one focus, they speed up when closer to the Sun, and a planet's orbital period is mathematically linked to its distance from the Sun. NASA's Kepler space telescope was named after him."],
  ["when did galileo live | galileo galilei facts | fun facts about galileo", "**Galileo Galilei** (1564–1642) was an Italian scientist. He improved the telescope and in 1610 discovered four moons of Jupiter, the phases of Venus and craters on the Moon. His support for a Sun-centered solar system got him put under house arrest by the Church for the last years of his life."],
  ["when was isaac newton born | newton facts | fun facts about isaac newton", "**Isaac Newton** (1642–1727) was born in England on Christmas Day 1642. Besides gravity and his three laws of motion, he co-invented calculus, discovered that white light is made of a rainbow of colors, and built the first practical reflecting telescope."],
  ["what are newtons three laws | newton's laws of motion | what are newton's laws", "**Newton's three laws of motion** (1687): 1) An object stays at rest or keeps moving in a straight line unless a force acts on it. 2) Force equals mass times acceleration (F = ma). 3) For every action there is an equal and opposite reaction."],
  ["who was gottfried leibniz | who was leibniz | tell me about leibniz", "**Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz** (1646–1716) was a German mathematician and philosopher. He invented calculus independently of Isaac Newton, and the calculus symbols we use today (like dx and the ∫ sign) are his. He also worked on the binary number system used by computers."],
  ["who invented calculus | who discovered calculus", "**Calculus** was developed independently by **Isaac Newton** in England and **Gottfried Leibniz** in Germany in the late 1600s. They had a famous argument over who was first; today both get credit, and we use Leibniz's notation."],
  ["who was blaise pascal | who was pascal | tell me about blaise pascal", "**Blaise Pascal** (1623–1662) was a French mathematician, scientist and writer. As a teenager he built one of the first mechanical calculators, and he did important work on probability and air pressure. The unit of pressure, the **pascal**, is named after him."],
  ["who was robert hooke | tell me about robert hooke", "**Robert Hooke** (1635–1703) was an English scientist who looked at cork through a microscope and named the tiny compartments he saw **cells**. His 1665 book *Micrographia* amazed readers with detailed drawings of fleas and other tiny things. He also described how springs stretch (Hooke's law)."],
  ["who was antonie van leeuwenhoek | who was van leeuwenhoek | tell me about leeuwenhoek", "**Antonie van Leeuwenhoek** (1632–1723) was a Dutch cloth merchant who built powerful single-lens microscopes. He was the first person to see bacteria and other microorganisms, which he called \"animalcules,\" and is called the \"father of microbiology.\""],
  ["who was carl linnaeus | tell me about linnaeus | carl linnaeus", "**Carl Linnaeus** (1707–1778) was a Swedish botanist who created the modern system for naming living things. He gave each species a two-part Latin name, like *Homo sapiens* for humans, a system called **binomial nomenclature**."],
  ["who was leonhard euler | who was euler | tell me about euler", "**Leonhard Euler** (1707–1783) was a Swiss mathematician and one of the most productive mathematicians ever. He introduced the symbols *e*, *i* and *f(x)*, popularized π, and kept publishing important work even after going nearly blind."],
  ["who was carl friedrich gauss | who was gauss | tell me about gauss", "**Carl Friedrich Gauss** (1777–1855) was a German mathematician called the \"prince of mathematicians.\" As a schoolboy he reportedly added the numbers 1 to 100 in seconds by pairing them up, and he made major discoveries in number theory, statistics and magnetism."],
  ["who was antoine lavoisier | who was lavoisier | tell me about lavoisier", "**Antoine Lavoisier** (1743–1794) was a French chemist known as the \"father of modern chemistry.\" He named oxygen and hydrogen, showed that burning is a reaction with oxygen, and showed that mass is conserved in chemical reactions."],
  ["who was edward jenner | tell me about edward jenner | edward jenner", "**Edward Jenner** (1749–1823) was an English doctor who created the world's first vaccine in 1796. He noticed that milkmaids who caught mild cowpox didn't get deadly smallpox, and used cowpox to protect people. His work eventually led to smallpox being wiped out in 1980."],
  ["who was alessandro volta | who was volta | tell me about alessandro volta", "**Alessandro Volta** (1745–1827) was an Italian physicist who invented the first electric battery, the **voltaic pile**, in 1800. The unit of electrical potential, the **volt**, is named after him."],
  ["what is michael faraday famous for | faraday inventions | what did faraday invent", "**Michael Faraday** (1791–1867) is famous for discovering **electromagnetic induction**, which is how electric generators and transformers work. He also built the first electric motor, despite having almost no formal schooling, and loved giving science lectures for children."],
  ["who was james clerk maxwell | who was maxwell | tell me about james clerk maxwell", "**James Clerk Maxwell** (1831–1879) was a Scottish physicist. He combined electricity, magnetism and light into a single theory, and showed that light is an electromagnetic wave."],
  ["what is maxwell famous for | what are maxwell's equations | what did james clerk maxwell discover", "**Maxwell** is famous for **Maxwell's equations**, four equations that describe how electric and magnetic fields work. They predicted radio waves, and Einstein called Maxwell's work the most profound since Newton. Maxwell also took the first color photograph in 1861."],
  ["charles darwin facts | when did charles darwin live | what was the hms beagle", "**Charles Darwin** (1809–1882) was an English naturalist. From 1831 to 1836 he sailed around the world on the ship **HMS Beagle**, studying animals like the finches and giant tortoises of the Galápagos Islands. In 1859 he published *On the Origin of Species*."],
  ["what did gregor mendel discover | mendel pea plants | what is mendel famous for", "**Gregor Mendel** (1822–1884) was a monk in what is now the Czech Republic who bred thousands of pea plants. He discovered that traits like flower color are passed down in predictable patterns, laying the foundation of **genetics**, though his work was ignored until 1900."],
  ["what is louis pasteur famous for | pasteur germ theory | when did louis pasteur live", "**Louis Pasteur** (1822–1895) was a French chemist famous for proving that germs cause disease and spoilage. He invented **pasteurization** to keep milk and wine safe and developed vaccines for rabies and anthrax."],
  ["who was dmitri mendeleev | tell me about mendeleev", "**Dmitri Mendeleev** (1834–1907) was a Russian chemist who created the **periodic table** in 1869. He arranged the elements by weight and properties and left gaps for elements not yet discovered, correctly predicting what they would be like."],
  ["who was joseph lister | tell me about joseph lister", "**Joseph Lister** (1827–1912) was a British surgeon who pioneered **antiseptic surgery**. Inspired by Pasteur's germ theory, he cleaned wounds and instruments with carbolic acid, which greatly reduced deaths from infection after operations."],
  ["who was robert koch | tell me about robert koch", "**Robert Koch** (1843–1910) was a German doctor and a founder of modern bacteriology. He identified the bacteria that cause tuberculosis, cholera and anthrax, and won the Nobel Prize in 1905."],
  ["marie curie facts | fun facts about marie curie | when did marie curie live", "**Marie Curie** (1867–1934) was born in Warsaw, Poland, and worked in Paris. She was the first woman to win a Nobel Prize and is still the only person to win Nobel Prizes in two different sciences (physics 1903 and chemistry 1911). She named the element **polonium** after her home country."],
  ["who was wilhelm roentgen | who was wilhelm röntgen | who discovered x-rays first", "**Wilhelm Röntgen** (1845–1923) was a German physicist who discovered **X-rays** in 1895. One of the first X-ray images was of his wife's hand, showing her bones and ring. He won the very first Nobel Prize in Physics in 1901."],
  ["who was max planck | tell me about max planck", "**Max Planck** (1858–1947) was a German physicist who started **quantum theory** in 1900 when he proposed that energy comes in tiny packets called quanta. He won the 1918 Nobel Prize in Physics."],
  ["who was ernest rutherford | who was rutherford | tell me about rutherford", "**Ernest Rutherford** (1871–1937) was a New Zealand-born physicist called the \"father of nuclear physics.\" His 1911 gold foil experiment showed that atoms have a tiny, dense **nucleus** at the center, and he later discovered the proton."],
  ["einstein facts | fun facts about albert einstein | when was einstein born", "**Albert Einstein** (1879–1955) was born in Ulm, Germany, and later became an American citizen. He published four groundbreaking papers in 1905 while working at a patent office, won the Nobel Prize for explaining the photoelectric effect, and loved playing the violin."],
  ["what is e=mc2 | what does e mc squared mean | what is einstein's famous equation", "**E = mc²** is Einstein's 1905 equation saying energy (E) equals mass (m) times the speed of light (c) squared. Because the speed of light is so huge, a tiny bit of mass holds an enormous amount of energy, which is how the Sun shines."],
  ["who was niels bohr | who was bohr | tell me about niels bohr", "**Niels Bohr** (1885–1962) was a Danish physicist who created the **Bohr model** of the atom in 1913, with electrons orbiting the nucleus at fixed energy levels. He won the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1922 and was a key founder of quantum mechanics."],
  ["who was erwin schrodinger | who was schrödinger | what is schrodinger's cat", "**Erwin Schrödinger** (1887–1961) was an Austrian physicist who wrote the **Schrödinger equation**, which describes how quantum particles behave. His famous \"cat in a box\" was a thought experiment (no real cat!) showing how strange quantum rules seem when applied to everyday objects."],
  ["who was werner heisenberg | who was heisenberg | what is the uncertainty principle", "**Werner Heisenberg** (1901–1976) was a German physicist and a founder of quantum mechanics. His **uncertainty principle** says you can't know both the exact position and the exact speed of a tiny particle at the same time."],
  ["who was enrico fermi | tell me about enrico fermi", "**Enrico Fermi** (1901–1954) was an Italian-American physicist who built the world's first nuclear reactor, **Chicago Pile-1**, in 1942. He won the 1938 Nobel Prize in Physics, and the element fermium is named after him."],
  ["who was robert oppenheimer | who was j robert oppenheimer | tell me about oppenheimer", "**J. Robert Oppenheimer** (1904–1967) was an American physicist who led the Los Alamos laboratory during World War II, where the first atomic bombs were built. He is often called the \"father of the atomic bomb\" and later spoke out about the dangers of nuclear weapons."],
  ["who was lise meitner | tell me about lise meitner", "**Lise Meitner** (1878–1968) was an Austrian-Swedish physicist who helped discover **nuclear fission**, the splitting of the atom, in 1938. Her partner Otto Hahn got the Nobel Prize, but Meitner was later honored with the element **meitnerium**."],
  ["who was alexander fleming | tell me about alexander fleming | alexander fleming", "**Alexander Fleming** (1881–1955) was a Scottish scientist who discovered **penicillin** in 1928 after noticing mold killing bacteria in a dish he'd left out. Penicillin became the first widely used antibiotic and has saved millions of lives."],
  ["who was edwin hubble | tell me about edwin hubble | what did hubble discover", "**Edwin Hubble** (1889–1953) was an American astronomer who proved that there are other galaxies beyond our Milky Way, and found that the universe is **expanding**. The Hubble Space Telescope is named after him."],
  ["what is nikola tesla famous for | tesla alternating current | when did nikola tesla live", "**Nikola Tesla** (1856–1943) was a Serbian-American inventor famous for developing **alternating current (AC)** electricity and the induction motor, which power homes and factories today. He also experimented with wireless power and radio, and the unit of magnetic field strength, the tesla, is named after him."],
  ["what is thomas edison famous for | how many patents did edison have | when did thomas edison live", "**Thomas Edison** (1847–1931) was an American inventor who held more than **1,000 patents**. He's famous for the phonograph, a long-lasting practical light bulb, the motion picture camera and building the first industrial research lab in Menlo Park, New Jersey."],
  ["who was alexander graham bell | tell me about alexander graham bell | alexander graham bell", "**Alexander Graham Bell** (1847–1922) was a Scottish-born inventor and teacher of deaf students who lived in the U.S. and Canada. He received the first U.S. patent for the **telephone** in 1876."],
  ["what did alexander graham bell invent | what was the first phone call | what is alexander graham bell famous for", "**Alexander Graham Bell** is famous for the **telephone**. The first phone call, in 1876, was to his assistant: \"Mr. Watson, come here, I want to see you.\" He also worked on hydrofoil boats, kites and devices to help deaf people."],
  ["who was guglielmo marconi | who was marconi | tell me about marconi", "**Guglielmo Marconi** (1874–1937) was an Italian inventor who developed long-distance **radio** communication. In 1901 he sent a radio signal across the Atlantic Ocean, and he shared the 1909 Nobel Prize in Physics."],
  ["tell me about the wright brothers | wright brothers", "**Wilbur Wright** (1867–1912) and **Orville Wright** (1871–1948) were American brothers who ran a bicycle shop in Dayton, Ohio. On December 17, 1903, at Kitty Hawk, North Carolina, they made the first controlled, powered airplane flight."],
  ["how long was the first airplane flight | what did the wright brothers invent | what are the wright brothers famous for", "The **Wright brothers** are famous for inventing the first successful airplane, the **Wright Flyer**. Orville flew it first on December 17, 1903, for 12 seconds and about 120 feet (37 meters). Their key idea was a way to steer by twisting the wings, called wing-warping."],
  ["who was johannes gutenberg | tell me about gutenberg", "**Johannes Gutenberg** (c. 1400–1468) was a German goldsmith and inventor who built the first movable-type **printing press** in Europe around 1440. His press made books much cheaper and faster to produce."],
  ["what is the gutenberg bible | what is gutenberg famous for", "**Gutenberg** is famous for the **Gutenberg Bible**, printed around 1455, one of the first major books made with movable metal type. His printing press helped spread knowledge, literacy and new ideas across Europe."],
  ["who was james watt | tell me about james watt", "**James Watt** (1736–1819) was a Scottish inventor who greatly improved the **steam engine** in the 1760s and 1770s, making it far more efficient. His engines powered the Industrial Revolution, and the unit of power, the **watt**, is named after him."],
  ["who was samuel morse | tell me about samuel morse | what is morse code", "**Samuel Morse** (1791–1872) was an American painter and inventor who helped develop the electric **telegraph** and **Morse code**, a system of dots and dashes for letters. In 1844 he sent the message \"What hath God wrought\" from Washington to Baltimore."],
  ["tell me about alfred nobel | what is the nobel prize", "**Alfred Nobel** (1833–1896) was a Swedish chemist who invented dynamite. In his will he left most of his fortune to create the **Nobel Prizes**, awarded since 1901 for physics, chemistry, medicine, literature and peace (economics was added later)."],
  ["who was louis braille | tell me about louis braille | who invented braille", "**Louis Braille** (1809–1852) was a Frenchman who became blind after an accident at age 3. When he was only 15 he invented **braille**, a reading and writing system of raised dots that blind people read with their fingertips."],
  ["who was karl benz | tell me about karl benz", "**Karl Benz** (1844–1929) was a German engineer who built the **Benz Patent-Motorwagen** in 1885, widely considered the first practical gasoline-powered car. His wife **Bertha Benz** took it on the first long-distance car trip in 1888."],
  ["who was george washington carver | tell me about george washington carver | george washington carver", "**George Washington Carver** (c. 1864–1943) was an American scientist and inventor, born into slavery in Missouri. He became a famous professor at the Tuskegee Institute, where he taught farmers how to improve their soil."],
  ["what is george washington carver famous for | did george washington carver invent peanut butter", "**George Washington Carver** is famous for promoting crop rotation and finding hundreds of uses for **peanuts**, sweet potatoes and soybeans. He didn't actually invent peanut butter, which existed before him, but he helped make peanuts a major crop in the American South."],
  ["what is stephen hawking famous for | when did stephen hawking live | what was stephen hawking's illness", "**Stephen Hawking** (1942–2018) was famous for showing that black holes give off radiation and for his bestselling book *A Brief History of Time*. He had **ALS**, a disease that slowly paralyzed him, and spoke using a computer voice he controlled with a cheek muscle."],
  ["what is alan turing famous for | when did alan turing live", "**Alan Turing** (1912–1954) was a British mathematician famous for helping crack the German **Enigma** code in World War II and for laying the foundations of computer science. His **Turing test** asks whether a machine can chat so well that a person can't tell it isn't human."],
  ["what is ada lovelace famous for | when did ada lovelace live | ada lovelace analytical engine", "**Ada Lovelace** (1815–1852) is famous for her 1843 notes on Charles Babbage's Analytical Engine, which included a step-by-step method for it to calculate numbers, often called the first computer program. She also imagined that computers might one day make music. She was the daughter of the poet Lord Byron."],
  ["who was charles babbage | tell me about charles babbage | charles babbage", "**Charles Babbage** (1791–1871) was an English mathematician called the \"father of the computer.\" He designed the **Difference Engine** and the **Analytical Engine**, mechanical calculating machines that had the main parts of a modern computer, though he never finished building them."],
  ["who was grace hopper | tell me about grace hopper | grace hopper", "**Grace Hopper** (1906–1992) was an American computer scientist and U.S. Navy rear admiral. She was one of the first programmers of the Harvard Mark I computer and earned the nickname \"Amazing Grace.\""],
  ["what is grace hopper famous for | did grace hopper find the first computer bug | what did grace hopper invent", "**Grace Hopper** is famous for creating one of the first **compilers**, which translate human-friendly code into machine code, and for helping develop the programming language **COBOL**. Her team famously found a real moth stuck in a computer in 1947 and taped it in the logbook as the \"first actual case of bug being found.\""],
  ["what is rosalind franklin famous for | what is photo 51 | when did rosalind franklin live", "**Rosalind Franklin** (1920–1958) was a British chemist famous for **Photo 51**, an X-ray image of DNA taken in 1952 that revealed its double-helix shape. Watson and Crick used her data to build their DNA model, and she is now widely recognized for her key role."],
  ["who were watson and crick | who was francis crick | who was james watson", "American biologist **James Watson** and British scientist **Francis Crick** (1916–2004), in 1953 at Cambridge, built the first correct model of DNA's **double helix**, using X-ray data from Rosalind Franklin and Maurice Wilkins. Watson, Crick and Wilkins won the Nobel Prize in 1962."],
  ["jane goodall chimpanzees | what did jane goodall discover about chimps | when did jane goodall live", "**Jane Goodall** (born 1934) studied wild chimpanzees at Gombe in Tanzania for decades starting in 1960. She discovered that chimps make and use tools, eat meat and have unique personalities, and she founded the Jane Goodall Institute to protect animals and nature."],
  ["who was rachel carson | tell me about rachel carson | rachel carson", "**Rachel Carson** (1907–1964) was an American marine biologist and writer. Her books about the ocean, like *The Sea Around Us*, made science beautiful to read, and she helped start the modern environmental movement."],
  ["what is rachel carson famous for | what is silent spring about", "**Rachel Carson** is famous for *Silent Spring* (1962), a book warning that pesticides like DDT were harming birds, wildlife and people. It led to new environmental laws and the creation of the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency."],
  ["what is cosmos by carl sagan | carl sagan facts | when did carl sagan live", "**Carl Sagan** (1934–1996) was an American astronomer who made space exciting for millions with his 1980 TV series *Cosmos*. He helped design the **Golden Records** carried by the Voyager spacecraft and coined the phrase \"pale blue dot\" for a photo of Earth from far away."],
  ["who was dian fossey | tell me about dian fossey", "**Dian Fossey** (1932–1985) was an American scientist who studied **mountain gorillas** in Rwanda for nearly 20 years. Her book *Gorillas in the Mist* and her fight against poaching helped save the gorillas from extinction."],
  ["who was barbara mcclintock | tell me about barbara mcclintock", "**Barbara McClintock** (1902–1992) was an American scientist who studied corn genetics and discovered **\"jumping genes\"** that can move around in DNA. She won the Nobel Prize in Medicine in 1983, the first woman to win it alone."],
  ["who was katherine johnson | tell me about katherine johnson | katherine johnson", "**Katherine Johnson** (1918–2020) was an African American mathematician at NASA whose calculations helped send astronaut John Glenn into orbit and Apollo 11 to the Moon. Her story was told in the book and film *Hidden Figures*."],
  ["what is hidden figures about | who were the hidden figures", "*Hidden Figures* tells the true story of three African American women mathematicians at NASA, **Katherine Johnson**, **Dorothy Vaughan** and **Mary Jackson**, whose work was vital to the U.S. space program in the 1960s."],
  ["who was richard feynman | tell me about richard feynman | richard feynman", "**Richard Feynman** (1918–1988) was an American physicist who won the Nobel Prize in 1965 for work on quantum electrodynamics. He was famous for his playful teaching style, Feynman diagrams, and explaining the cause of the 1986 Challenger shuttle disaster with a glass of ice water."],
  ["who was emmy noether | tell me about emmy noether", "**Emmy Noether** (1882–1935) was a German mathematician who made huge contributions to abstract algebra. **Noether's theorem** links symmetry to conservation laws in physics, and Einstein praised her as a creative mathematical genius."],
  ["who was srinivasa ramanujan | who was ramanujan | tell me about ramanujan", "**Srinivasa Ramanujan** (1887–1920) was an Indian mathematician who was largely self-taught. He discovered thousands of remarkable formulas, worked with G. H. Hardy at Cambridge, and the number **1729** is called the Hardy–Ramanujan number after a famous story about a taxi."],
  ["who was jonas salk | tell me about jonas salk | who invented the polio vaccine", "**Jonas Salk** (1914–1995) was an American doctor who developed the first successful **polio vaccine**, announced in 1955. He chose not to patent it so it could reach as many people as possible. **Albert Sabin** later developed an oral polio vaccine."],
  ["what is florence nightingale famous for | when did florence nightingale live", "**Florence Nightingale** (1820–1910) was a British nurse famous for improving hospital cleanliness during the Crimean War, which greatly reduced deaths. She founded a nursing school in London and used early charts and statistics to push for health reforms."],
  ["who was clara barton | tell me about clara barton | who founded the american red cross", "**Clara Barton** (1821–1912) was an American nurse who cared for wounded soldiers in the Civil War, earning the nickname \"Angel of the Battlefield.\" In 1881 she founded the **American Red Cross**."],
  ["who was elizabeth blackwell | tell me about elizabeth blackwell | who was the first female doctor in america", "**Elizabeth Blackwell** (1821–1910) was the first woman to earn a medical degree in the United States, in 1849. She opened a hospital for women and children in New York and helped other women become doctors."],
  ["who was mary anning | tell me about mary anning | mary anning", "**Mary Anning** (1799–1847) was an English fossil collector from Lyme Regis. As a child and young woman she found the first complete **ichthyosaur** and **plesiosaur** skeletons, and her discoveries changed how scientists thought about ancient life."],
  ["who was robert goddard | tell me about robert goddard", "**Robert Goddard** (1882–1945) was an American engineer called the \"father of modern rocketry.\" In 1926 he launched the world's first liquid-fueled rocket in Massachusetts. NASA's Goddard Space Flight Center is named after him."],
  ["who was robert boyle | tell me about robert boyle | what is boyles law", "**Robert Boyle** (1627–1691) was an Irish-born scientist and one of the founders of modern chemistry. **Boyle's law** says that if you squeeze a gas into a smaller space, its pressure goes up, as long as the temperature stays the same."],
  ["who was john dalton | tell me about john dalton", "**John Dalton** (1766–1844) was an English chemist who proposed modern **atomic theory** around 1803: that all matter is made of tiny atoms and each element has its own kind of atom. He also studied color blindness, which he had himself."],
  ["who was hedy lamarr | tell me about hedy lamarr | hedy lamarr", "**Hedy Lamarr** (1914–2000) was an Austrian-American movie star and inventor. During World War II she co-invented a **frequency-hopping** system for guiding torpedoes, an idea that later contributed to technologies like Wi-Fi and Bluetooth."],
  ["who was steve jobs | tell me about steve jobs | steve jobs", "**Steve Jobs** (1955–2011) co-founded **Apple** with Steve Wozniak in 1976. He led the creation of the Macintosh, iPod, iPhone and iPad, and also ran Pixar, the animation studio behind *Toy Story*."],
  ["what is henry ford famous for | when did henry ford live", "**Henry Ford** (1863–1947) founded the Ford Motor Company and is famous for the **Model T** (1908) and the moving **assembly line** (1913). These made cars cheap enough for ordinary families, changing how people lived and worked."],
  ["benjamin franklin kite experiment | what did benjamin franklin invent | benjamin franklin inventions", "**Benjamin Franklin** (1706–1790) flew a kite in a thunderstorm in 1752 to show that lightning is electricity (a very dangerous experiment!). He invented the **lightning rod**, bifocal glasses and the Franklin stove, and founded America's first lending library."],
  ["leonardo da vinci inventions | what did leonardo da vinci invent", "**Leonardo da Vinci** (1452–1519) filled notebooks with designs for flying machines, a parachute, an armored vehicle, diving gear and robots. Most were never built in his lifetime, but they show his amazing curiosity about how things work."],
  ["tell me about leif erikson | leif erikson", "**Leif Erikson** (c. 970–1020) was a Norse (Viking) explorer from Iceland. Around the year 1000 he reached North America, landing in a place he called **Vinland**, nearly 500 years before Columbus."],
  ["did vikings reach america first | who discovered america before columbus", "Yes. Norse explorers led by **Leif Erikson** reached North America around 1000 AD; remains of a Viking settlement were found at **L'Anse aux Meadows** in Newfoundland, Canada. And of course, Indigenous peoples had already lived in the Americas for more than 15,000 years."],
  ["when did marco polo live | what is marco polo famous for | marco polo travels", "**Marco Polo** (1254–1324) was a Venetian merchant famous for his journey along the Silk Road to China, where he spent about 17 years at the court of **Kublai Khan**. His book, *The Travels of Marco Polo*, gave Europeans a vivid picture of Asia."],
  ["who was ibn battuta | tell me about ibn battuta | ibn battuta", "**Ibn Battuta** (1304–c. 1369) was a Moroccan traveler who journeyed about 75,000 miles (120,000 km) over nearly 30 years, visiting North Africa, the Middle East, India, China and West Africa. His travel book, the *Rihla*, is a priceless record of the 14th-century world."],
  ["who was zheng he | tell me about zheng he | zheng he", "**Zheng He** (1371–1433) was a Chinese admiral who led seven huge sea voyages for the Ming dynasty between 1405 and 1433. His \"treasure fleet\" of enormous ships sailed as far as Arabia and East Africa."],
  ["what is christopher columbus famous for | when did christopher columbus live", "**Christopher Columbus** (1451–1506) was an Italian navigator sailing for Spain. He's famous for his 1492 voyage across the Atlantic with the *Niña*, *Pinta* and *Santa María*, which began lasting contact between Europe and the Americas. His arrival also brought great suffering to the Indigenous peoples already living there."],
  ["what is vasco da gama famous for | when did vasco da gama live", "**Vasco da Gama** (c. 1460–1524) was a Portuguese explorer famous for being the first European to reach **India by sea**, sailing around the southern tip of Africa in 1497–1498. His route opened up sea trade between Europe and Asia."],
  ["who was magellan | tell me about magellan", "**Ferdinand Magellan** (c. 1480–1521) was a Portuguese explorer who led a Spanish expedition that set out in 1519 to sail west to Asia. He found a passage around South America, now called the **Strait of Magellan**, and named the Pacific Ocean."],
  ["who was the first to sail around the world | first voyage around the world | first circumnavigation", "The first voyage around the world was **Magellan's expedition** (1519–1522). Magellan himself was killed in the Philippines in 1521, so **Juan Sebastián Elcano** led the one surviving ship, the *Victoria*, back to Spain with just 18 of the original crew."],
  ["who was amerigo vespucci | why is america called america | who was america named after", "**Amerigo Vespucci** (1454–1512) was an Italian explorer who sailed to South America around 1500 and realized it was a \"New World,\" not part of Asia. A mapmaker named the continents **America** after him in 1507."],
  ["who was james cook | who was captain cook | tell me about captain cook", "**Captain James Cook** (1728–1779) was a British explorer and navigator who made three long voyages across the Pacific Ocean. He mapped New Zealand and the east coast of Australia and made very accurate charts of many islands."],
  ["tell me about lewis and clark", "**Meriwether Lewis** (1774–1809) and **William Clark** (1770–1838) led an American expedition from 1804 to 1806 to explore the land of the Louisiana Purchase and reach the Pacific Ocean. Their Shoshone guide and interpreter **Sacagawea** was key to their success."],
  ["who was roald amundsen | tell me about roald amundsen | who reached the south pole first", "**Roald Amundsen** (1872–1928) was a Norwegian explorer who led the first expedition to reach the **South Pole**, on December 14, 1911, using dog sleds. He beat a British team led by Robert Falcon Scott by about five weeks."],
  ["who was ernest shackleton | tell me about shackleton | what happened to the endurance ship", "**Ernest Shackleton** (1874–1922) was an Irish-born British explorer of Antarctica. When his ship **Endurance** was crushed by ice in 1915, he led his stranded crew to safety on an incredible rescue journey, and every one of them survived. The shipwreck was found in 2022."],
  ["who was robert falcon scott | who was captain scott | tell me about robert scott", "**Robert Falcon Scott** (1868–1912) was a British naval officer who led an expedition to the South Pole. His team reached it in January 1912, only to find Amundsen had arrived first, and Scott and his companions died on the return journey."],
  ["who was matthew henson | tell me about matthew henson | who reached the north pole first", "**Matthew Henson** (1866–1955) was an African American explorer who traveled with **Robert Peary** on many Arctic expeditions. In 1909 their team claimed to be the first to reach the **North Pole**, though historians still debate exactly how close they got."],
  ["who was edmund hillary | who was tenzing norgay | who climbed mount everest first", "**Edmund Hillary** (1919–2008) of New Zealand and **Tenzing Norgay** (1914–1986), a Sherpa from Nepal, were the first people confirmed to reach the top of **Mount Everest**, on May 29, 1953."],
  ["who was jacques cousteau | tell me about jacques cousteau", "**Jacques Cousteau** (1910–1997) was a French ocean explorer, filmmaker and conservationist. He co-invented the **Aqua-Lung**, an early scuba system, in 1943, and his documentaries showed millions of people the wonders of the underwater world."],
  ["who was david livingstone | tell me about david livingstone | dr livingstone i presume", "**David Livingstone** (1813–1873) was a Scottish missionary and explorer of Africa who was the first European to see Victoria Falls. When the journalist Henry Morton Stanley found him in 1871, he supposedly greeted him with \"Dr. Livingstone, I presume?\""],
  ["who was henry hudson | tell me about henry hudson", "**Henry Hudson** (c. 1565–1611) was an English explorer who searched for a northern sea route to Asia. The **Hudson River** in New York and **Hudson Bay** in Canada are named after him."],
  ["who was francis drake | who was sir francis drake | tell me about francis drake", "**Sir Francis Drake** (c. 1540–1596) was an English sea captain who completed the second voyage around the world (1577–1580). He also helped defeat the Spanish Armada in 1588."],
  ["who was jacques cartier | tell me about jacques cartier", "**Jacques Cartier** (1491–1557) was a French explorer who sailed up the **St. Lawrence River** in the 1530s. He claimed the area for France, and the name \"Canada\" comes from a Huron-Iroquois word for village that he recorded."],
  ["what is neil armstrong famous for | when did neil armstrong live", "**Neil Armstrong** (1930–2012) was an American astronaut famous for being the first person to walk on the Moon, on July 20, 1969, during **Apollo 11**. As he stepped down he said, \"That's one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind.\""],
  ["what is yuri gagarin famous for | when did yuri gagarin live", "**Yuri Gagarin** (1934–1968) was a Soviet cosmonaut famous for becoming the first human in space. On April 12, 1961, he orbited Earth once in the **Vostok 1** spacecraft, a flight lasting about 108 minutes."],
  ["who was john glenn | tell me about john glenn | first american to orbit the earth", "**John Glenn** (1921–2016) was an American astronaut who became the first American to orbit Earth, in 1962. He later served as a U.S. senator and in 1998, at age 77, flew on the Space Shuttle, becoming the oldest person in space at the time."],
  ["what is amelia earhart famous for | when did amelia earhart live | what happened to amelia earhart", "**Amelia Earhart** (1897–1937) was an American pilot famous for being the first woman to fly solo across the Atlantic, in 1932. In 1937, while trying to fly around the world, she and navigator Fred Noonan disappeared over the Pacific Ocean, and what happened to them remains a mystery."],
  ["who was bessie coleman | tell me about bessie coleman", "**Bessie Coleman** (1892–1926) was the first African American woman and first Native American woman to earn a pilot's license, in 1921. She had to go to France to learn because U.S. flight schools wouldn't accept her, and she became a famous stunt pilot."],
  ["who was ramses ii | who was ramesses the great | tell me about ramses ii", "**Ramesses II** (c. 1303–1213 BC), also called Ramesses the Great, was one of ancient Egypt's most powerful pharaohs, ruling for about 66 years. He built huge monuments, including the temples at **Abu Simbel**."],
  ["who was hatshepsut | tell me about hatshepsut | hatshepsut", "**Hatshepsut** (c. 1507–1458 BC) was one of the few women to rule ancient Egypt as pharaoh. Her reign was peaceful and prosperous, she sent a famous trading expedition to the land of Punt, and she built a grand temple at Deir el-Bahari."],
  ["what is king tut famous for | why is tutankhamun famous", "**Tutankhamun** (c. 1341–1323 BC) was a young Egyptian pharaoh who became king at about age 9. He's famous mostly because his tomb was found nearly untouched by **Howard Carter** in 1922, packed with treasures including his gold burial mask."],
  ["who was howard carter | tell me about howard carter", "**Howard Carter** (1874–1939) was a British archaeologist who discovered the tomb of the pharaoh **Tutankhamun** in Egypt's Valley of the Kings in 1922. When asked if he could see anything, he reportedly said, \"Yes, wonderful things.\""],
  ["what is cleopatra famous for | when did cleopatra live | was cleopatra egyptian", "**Cleopatra VII** (69–30 BC) was the last active ruler of ancient Egypt's Ptolemaic kingdom. Her family was of Greek (Macedonian) descent, but she was the first in her dynasty to learn Egyptian. She's famous for her intelligence and her alliances with Julius Caesar and Mark Antony."],
  ["what is alexander the great famous for | when did alexander the great live | how big was alexander's empire", "**Alexander the Great** (356–323 BC) was a king of Macedonia who was taught by Aristotle. By age 30 he had built one of the largest empires of the ancient world, stretching from Greece to Egypt and India, and he never lost a battle."],
  ["what is julius caesar famous for | when did julius caesar live", "**Julius Caesar** (100–44 BC) was a Roman general and statesman famous for conquering Gaul (France), crossing the Rubicon river to take power in Rome, and creating the **Julian calendar**. The month of July is named after him."],
  ["what is augustus famous for | augustus caesar | when did augustus live", "**Augustus** (63 BC–14 AD), born Octavian, was Julius Caesar's adopted heir and the **first Roman emperor**. His rule began about 200 years of relative peace called the **Pax Romana**, and the month of August is named after him."],
  ["who was ashoka | who was ashoka the great | tell me about emperor ashoka", "**Ashoka** (c. 304–232 BC) was an emperor of the Maurya Empire in India. After a bloody war, he turned to Buddhism and peace, carving messages of kindness on stone pillars across his empire. His lion symbol is now India's national emblem."],
  ["what is qin shi huang famous for | who built the great wall of china", "**Qin Shi Huang** (259–210 BC) united China in 221 BC and became its first emperor. He standardized writing, money and measurements, began linking walls that became part of the **Great Wall**, and was buried with the **Terracotta Army**. The wall seen today was mostly built much later, by the Ming dynasty."],
  ["what is hannibal famous for | when did hannibal live", "**Hannibal Barca** (247–c. 183 BC) was a general from Carthage in North Africa, famous for crossing the **Alps** with an army and war elephants in 218 BC to attack Rome. He's still studied as one of history's greatest military tacticians."],
  ["who was attila the hun | tell me about attila the hun | attila the hun", "**Attila** (c. 406–453) was the ruler of the Huns, a nomadic people from Central Asia. He led fierce invasions of the Eastern and Western Roman Empires, earning the nickname \"the Scourge of God.\""],
  ["what is charlemagne famous for | when did charlemagne live", "**Charlemagne** (c. 742–814), or Charles the Great, was king of the Franks and was crowned **Emperor of the Romans** by the Pope in 800. He united much of Western Europe and encouraged learning, and he's often called the \"father of Europe.\""],
  ["tell me about william the conqueror", "**William the Conqueror** (c. 1028–1087) was a Duke of Normandy in France who invaded England and won the **Battle of Hastings** in 1066, becoming king of England. He ordered the **Domesday Book**, a huge survey of English land."],
  ["what is genghis khan famous for | when did genghis khan live", "**Genghis Khan** (c. 1162–1227) united the Mongol tribes and founded the **Mongol Empire**, which grew into the largest connected land empire in history. His fast horseback armies conquered much of Asia, and the empire boosted trade along the Silk Road."],
  ["who was kublai khan | tell me about kublai khan", "**Kublai Khan** (1215–1294) was the grandson of Genghis Khan. He completed the Mongol conquest of China and founded the **Yuan dynasty**, with its capital at what is now Beijing. Marco Polo described visiting his court."],
  ["who was saladin | tell me about saladin", "**Saladin** (1137–1193) was a Muslim sultan of Egypt and Syria of Kurdish origin. He recaptured Jerusalem in 1187 during the Crusades and was respected even by his enemies for his chivalry and fairness."],
  ["who was richard the lionheart | tell me about richard the lionheart", "**Richard I of England** (1157–1199), called Richard the Lionheart, was a king famous for his bravery as a military leader in the Third Crusade, where he fought against Saladin. He actually spent only about six months of his 10-year reign in England."],
  ["who was mansa musa | tell me about mansa musa | who was the richest person ever", "**Mansa Musa** (c. 1280–1337) was the ruler of the **Mali Empire** in West Africa, often called one of the richest people in history thanks to Mali's gold. On his pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324 he gave away so much gold that its value dropped in Cairo for years."],
  ["who was suleiman the magnificent | tell me about suleiman the magnificent", "**Suleiman the Magnificent** (1494–1566) was the longest-reigning sultan of the **Ottoman Empire**, which reached its peak under him. He was known in his empire as \"the Lawgiver\" for reforming its laws, and he supported great art and architecture."],
  ["who was akbar | who was akbar the great | tell me about akbar", "**Akbar the Great** (1542–1605) was an emperor of the **Mughal Empire** in India. He expanded the empire, reformed taxes, and was known for welcoming scholars from many religions to discuss ideas at his court."],
  ["who was shah jahan | why was the taj mahal built", "The **Taj Mahal** in Agra, India, was built by the Mughal emperor **Shah Jahan** (1592–1666) as a tomb for his beloved wife **Mumtaz Mahal**, who died in 1631. It took about 20 years and some 20,000 workers to complete."],
  ["what is joan of arc famous for | when did joan of arc live", "**Joan of Arc** (c. 1412–1431) was a French peasant girl who, guided by what she believed were holy voices, led the French army to lift the **Siege of Orléans** in 1429 during the Hundred Years' War. She was captured and executed at about 19, and was later declared a saint."],
  ["what is henry viii famous for | when did henry viii live", "**Henry VIII** (1491–1547) was king of England, famous for having **six wives** and for breaking away from the Roman Catholic Church to create the **Church of England**, partly so he could end his first marriage."],
  ["what is queen elizabeth i famous for | when did elizabeth i live | who was the virgin queen", "**Elizabeth I** (1533–1603) was queen of England for 44 years, a time called the **Elizabethan era** when theater, poetry and exploration flourished. She defeated the Spanish Armada in 1588, never married, and was nicknamed \"the Virgin Queen.\""],
  ["who was peter the great | tell me about peter the great", "**Peter the Great** (1672–1725) was a Russian tsar who modernized Russia by bringing in Western European science, technology and customs. He built a navy and founded the city of **St. Petersburg** in 1703 as his new capital."],
  ["who was catherine the great | tell me about catherine the great", "**Catherine the Great** (1729–1796) was Russia's longest-ruling female leader, empress from 1762 to 1796. Born a German princess, she expanded the Russian Empire, supported art and education, and corresponded with Enlightenment thinkers like Voltaire."],
  ["who was louis xiv | who was the sun king | tell me about louis xiv", "**Louis XIV** (1638–1715), the \"Sun King,\" ruled France for 72 years, the longest reign of any major European monarch. He built the magnificent **Palace of Versailles** and made France the leading power in Europe."],
  ["who was marie antoinette | tell me about marie antoinette | did marie antoinette say let them eat cake", "**Marie Antoinette** (1755–1793) was the Austrian-born queen of France, married to King Louis XVI. She was executed during the French Revolution. There's no evidence she ever said \"Let them eat cake\"; that story was told about others before her."],
  ["what is napoleon famous for | when did napoleon live", "**Napoleon Bonaparte** (1769–1821) was a French general who crowned himself emperor in 1804 and conquered much of Europe before his final defeat at **Waterloo** in 1815. His **Napoleonic Code** shaped laws worldwide. He wasn't really short: about 5 feet 7 inches, average for his time."],
  ["what is queen victoria famous for | when did queen victoria live", "**Queen Victoria** (1819–1901) reigned over the United Kingdom for 63 years, during which Britain's empire and industry grew enormously. The period is called the **Victorian era**, and she had nine children who married into royal families across Europe."],
  ["what is george washington famous for | when did george washington live | did george washington have wooden teeth", "**George Washington** (1732–1799) led the Continental Army to victory in the American Revolutionary War and became the **first U.S. president** (1789–1797). He chose to step down after two terms. His false teeth weren't wood; they were made of ivory, metal and even human and animal teeth."],
  ["what is thomas jefferson famous for | when did thomas jefferson live", "**Thomas Jefferson** (1743–1826) is famous for writing the **Declaration of Independence** in 1776 and serving as the third U.S. president. He arranged the **Louisiana Purchase**, sent Lewis and Clark west, and founded the University of Virginia."],
  ["what is abraham lincoln famous for | when did abraham lincoln live | what was the gettysburg address", "**Abraham Lincoln** (1809–1865) was the 16th U.S. president, famous for leading the country through the Civil War, issuing the **Emancipation Proclamation** in 1863 and giving the short but powerful **Gettysburg Address**, which begins \"Four score and seven years ago.\""],
  ["who was john adams | tell me about john adams", "**John Adams** (1735–1826) was a Founding Father who helped lead the American Revolution, served as the first U.S. vice president, and became the **second president** (1797–1801). His son John Quincy Adams became the sixth president."],
  ["who was theodore roosevelt | who was teddy roosevelt | tell me about teddy roosevelt", "**Theodore Roosevelt** (1858–1919) was the 26th U.S. president (1901–1909). A lover of the outdoors, he protected about 230 million acres of land as national forests, parks and wildlife refuges. The **teddy bear** is named after him."],
  ["who was franklin d roosevelt | who was fdr | tell me about fdr", "**Franklin D. Roosevelt** (1882–1945), known as FDR, was the 32nd U.S. president and the only one elected four times. He led the country through the Great Depression with his **New Deal** programs and through most of World War II."],
  ["who was eleanor roosevelt | tell me about eleanor roosevelt", "**Eleanor Roosevelt** (1884–1962) was First Lady of the United States from 1933 to 1945 and a champion of human rights. She led the committee that wrote the United Nations' **Universal Declaration of Human Rights** in 1948."],
  ["who was john f kennedy | who was jfk | tell me about jfk", "**John F. Kennedy** (1917–1963) was the 35th U.S. president. He started the Peace Corps, set the goal of landing a man on the Moon, and handled the Cuban Missile Crisis in 1962. He was assassinated in Dallas, Texas, in November 1963."],
  ["what is winston churchill famous for | when did winston churchill live", "**Winston Churchill** (1874–1965) was British Prime Minister during most of World War II, famous for speeches like \"We shall fight on the beaches\" and his refusal to surrender to Nazi Germany. He was also a painter and won the 1953 Nobel Prize in Literature."],
  ["what is gandhi famous for | when did gandhi live | what is nonviolent resistance", "**Mahatma Gandhi** (1869–1948) led India's independence movement against British rule using **nonviolent resistance**: peaceful protests, boycotts and marches like the 1930 **Salt March**. His methods inspired civil rights leaders around the world, including Martin Luther King Jr."],
  ["what is nelson mandela famous for | when did nelson mandela live | how long was mandela in prison", "**Nelson Mandela** (1918–2013) fought against apartheid, South Africa's system of racial segregation, and spent **27 years in prison**. After his release in 1990 he worked for peaceful change, won the Nobel Peace Prize, and became South Africa's first Black president in 1994."],
  ["what is martin luther king jr famous for | i have a dream speech | when did martin luther king jr live", "**Martin Luther King Jr.** (1929–1968) was a Baptist minister who led the American civil rights movement using nonviolent protest. He's famous for his **\"I Have a Dream\"** speech at the 1963 March on Washington, and he won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1964."],
  ["what is rosa parks famous for | when did rosa parks live | montgomery bus boycott", "**Rosa Parks** (1913–2005) is famous for refusing to give up her bus seat to a white passenger in Montgomery, Alabama, on December 1, 1955. Her arrest sparked the **Montgomery Bus Boycott**, a key moment in the civil rights movement."],
  ["what is harriet tubman famous for | when did harriet tubman live", "**Harriet Tubman** (c. 1822–1913) escaped slavery in 1849 and then returned to the South about 13 times to guide around 70 people to freedom on the **Underground Railroad**. During the Civil War she served as a scout and spy for the Union Army."],
  ["what is frederick douglass famous for | when did frederick douglass live", "**Frederick Douglass** (c. 1818–1895) escaped slavery and became a powerful speaker and writer against it. His autobiography, *Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass*, showed the cruelty of slavery, and he later advised President Lincoln."],
  ["who was sojourner truth | tell me about sojourner truth | aint i a woman speech", "**Sojourner Truth** (c. 1797–1883) was an African American abolitionist and women's rights activist who escaped slavery in New York. She's famous for her 1851 speech at a women's rights convention, remembered as **\"Ain't I a Woman?\"**"],
  ["tell me about susan b anthony", "**Susan B. Anthony** (1820–1906) was an American leader in the fight for **women's right to vote**. She was arrested in 1872 for voting, and the 19th Amendment giving women the vote, passed in 1920, was nicknamed the \"Susan B. Anthony Amendment.\""],
  ["who was elizabeth cady stanton | tell me about elizabeth cady stanton", "**Elizabeth Cady Stanton** (1815–1902) was an American women's rights leader who helped organize the first women's rights convention at **Seneca Falls**, New York, in 1848, where she wrote the Declaration of Sentiments calling for women's equality."],
  ["who was emmeline pankhurst | tell me about emmeline pankhurst", "**Emmeline Pankhurst** (1858–1928) was a British activist who led the **suffragettes**, campaigning for women's right to vote. British women over 30 won the vote in 1918, and all women over 21 won it in 1928, just after her death."],
  ["who was booker t washington | tell me about booker t washington", "**Booker T. Washington** (1856–1915) was born into slavery and became a leading African American educator. In 1881 he became the first leader of the **Tuskegee Institute** in Alabama, and he wrote the autobiography *Up from Slavery*."],
  ["who was web du bois | who was w e b du bois | tell me about web du bois", "**W. E. B. Du Bois** (1868–1963) was an African American scholar and civil rights activist. He was the first Black American to earn a PhD from Harvard, co-founded the **NAACP** in 1909, and wrote *The Souls of Black Folk*."],
  ["who was thurgood marshall | tell me about thurgood marshall", "**Thurgood Marshall** (1908–1993) was a civil rights lawyer who won **Brown v. Board of Education** (1954), which ended legal school segregation in the U.S. In 1967 he became the first African American Supreme Court justice."],
  ["who was malcolm x | tell me about malcolm x", "**Malcolm X** (1925–1965) was an African American minister and civil rights leader who spoke powerfully about Black pride and self-reliance. His autobiography, written with Alex Haley, is one of the most influential American books of the 20th century."],
  ["who was cesar chavez | tell me about cesar chavez", "**César Chávez** (1927–1993) was a Mexican American labor leader who co-founded the **United Farm Workers** with Dolores Huerta. He used nonviolent strikes, boycotts and fasting to win better pay and conditions for farm workers."],
  ["who was mother teresa | tell me about mother teresa", "**Mother Teresa** (1910–1997) was a Catholic nun, born in what is now North Macedonia, who spent most of her life caring for the poor and sick in **Kolkata**, India. She founded the Missionaries of Charity and won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1979."],
  ["who was simon bolivar | tell me about simon bolivar | simon bolivar", "**Simón Bolívar** (1783–1830) was a Venezuelan military and political leader known as **\"El Libertador.\"** He led the fight for independence from Spain for Venezuela, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru and Bolivia, which is named after him."],
  ["who was toussaint louverture | tell me about toussaint louverture", "**Toussaint Louverture** (c. 1743–1803) was a formerly enslaved man who became the leader of the **Haitian Revolution**. The revolt he helped lead made Haiti, in 1804, the first nation founded by people who freed themselves from slavery."],
  ["who was shaka zulu | tell me about shaka zulu", "**Shaka** (c. 1787–1828) was a king of the **Zulu** people in southern Africa. He reorganized the Zulu army with new weapons and tactics and built the Zulu kingdom into a major power."],
  ["who was montezuma | who was moctezuma | tell me about montezuma", "**Moctezuma II** (c. 1466–1520) was the emperor of the **Aztec Empire**, ruling from its capital **Tenochtitlan** (now Mexico City). His empire fell to Spanish conquistadors led by Hernán Cortés and their Indigenous allies in 1521."],
  ["who was pachacuti | who founded the inca empire", "**Pachacuti** (died c. 1471) was the Inca ruler who transformed a small kingdom into the vast **Inca Empire** in the 1400s. **Machu Picchu** in Peru is believed to have been built as a royal estate for him."],
  ["who was sitting bull | tell me about sitting bull", "**Sitting Bull** (c. 1831–1890) was a Hunkpapa Lakota leader who resisted U.S. government efforts to take his people's land. He was a key leader at the **Battle of the Little Bighorn** in 1876, where Lakota and Cheyenne warriors defeated General Custer."],
  ["who was crazy horse | tell me about crazy horse", "**Crazy Horse** (c. 1840–1877) was an Oglala Lakota war leader who fought to protect his people's land and way of life. He was a key leader at the **Battle of the Little Bighorn** in 1876, and a giant mountain carving honoring him is being made in South Dakota."],
  ["who was tecumseh | tell me about tecumseh", "**Tecumseh** (1768–1813) was a Shawnee leader who tried to unite many Native American nations to resist settlers taking their lands. He fought alongside the British in the War of 1812 and was killed in battle in 1813."],
  ["who was geronimo | tell me about geronimo", "**Geronimo** (1829–1909) was a Bedonkohe Apache leader who fought for years against Mexican and U.S. forces trying to take Apache lands. He surrendered in 1886 and spent his later years as a prisoner of war."],
  ["who was pocahontas | tell me about pocahontas | was pocahontas real", "**Pocahontas** (c. 1596–1617) was a real Powhatan woman, the daughter of a powerful chief in what is now Virginia. She interacted with English settlers at Jamestown, later married colonist John Rolfe, and traveled to England, where she died young. The famous romance with John Smith is likely a legend."],
  ["who was mustafa kemal ataturk | who was ataturk | tell me about ataturk", "**Mustafa Kemal Atatürk** (1881–1938) was the founder of modern **Turkey** and its first president, from 1923. He introduced sweeping reforms, including a new Latin-based alphabet, and his surname means \"Father of the Turks.\""],
  ["who was jawaharlal nehru | who was nehru | tell me about nehru", "**Jawaharlal Nehru** (1889–1964) was a leader of India's independence movement alongside Gandhi and became India's **first prime minister** in 1947, serving until his death. His birthday is celebrated as Children's Day in India."],
  ["who was otto von bismarck | who was bismarck | tell me about bismarck", "**Otto von Bismarck** (1815–1898) was a Prussian statesman who united the German states into a single **German Empire** in 1871 and served as its first chancellor. He was nicknamed the \"Iron Chancellor.\""],
  ["who was giuseppe garibaldi | who was garibaldi | tell me about garibaldi", "**Giuseppe Garibaldi** (1807–1882) was an Italian general and patriot who helped unify Italy in the 1800s. He led a famous volunteer army called the \"Redshirts\" that conquered Sicily and Naples in 1860."],
  ["who was benito juarez | tell me about benito juarez", "**Benito Juárez** (1806–1872) was a Zapotec lawyer who became president of **Mexico** and its first Indigenous president. He resisted the French invasion of Mexico and is honored as a national hero for his reforms and defense of the law."],
  ["who was wangari maathai | tell me about wangari maathai", "**Wangari Maathai** (1940–2011) was a Kenyan environmentalist who founded the **Green Belt Movement**, which has planted tens of millions of trees in Africa. In 2004 she became the first African woman to win the Nobel Peace Prize."],
  ["what is anne frank famous for | when did anne frank live", "**Anne Frank** (1929–1945) was a Jewish girl who hid with her family in a secret annex in Amsterdam during World War II. She is famous for the diary she kept while in hiding, published by her father after the war as *The Diary of a Young Girl*."],
  ["what is helen keller famous for | when did helen keller live | who was anne sullivan", "**Helen Keller** (1880–1968) became deaf and blind as a baby. With her teacher **Anne Sullivan**, who spelled words into her hand, she learned to communicate, graduated from college in 1904, and became a famous author and activist for people with disabilities."],
  ["who was princess diana | tell me about princess diana", "**Diana, Princess of Wales** (1961–1997) was the first wife of King Charles III and the mother of Princes William and Harry. Known as the \"People's Princess,\" she was admired for her charity work, especially helping people with HIV/AIDS and campaigning against landmines."],
  ["what is paul revere famous for | one if by land two if by sea", "**Paul Revere** (1735–1818) was a Boston silversmith famous for his midnight ride on April 18, 1775, warning colonists that British troops were coming. The signal \"one if by land, two if by sea\" referred to lanterns hung in a church steeple to show the British route."],
  ["what is alexander hamilton famous for | when did alexander hamilton live", "**Alexander Hamilton** (c. 1755–1804) was a Founding Father and the first U.S. Secretary of the Treasury, who set up the nation's financial system. He co-wrote *The Federalist Papers*, died after a duel with Aaron Burr, and is the subject of the musical *Hamilton*."],
  ["what is leonardo da vinci famous for | when did leonardo da vinci live", "**Leonardo da Vinci** (1452–1519) was an Italian Renaissance genius famous for painting the **Mona Lisa** and **The Last Supper**. He was also an inventor, engineer and scientist who studied anatomy, flight and water, filling thousands of notebook pages written in mirror writing."],
  ["what is michelangelo famous for | when did michelangelo live", "**Michelangelo Buonarroti** (1475–1564) was an Italian Renaissance artist famous for the marble statue **David**, the **Sistine Chapel ceiling** paintings in Rome, and designing the dome of St. Peter's Basilica. He thought of himself mainly as a sculptor."],
  ["who was raphael | who was raphael the painter | tell me about raphael", "**Raphael** (1483–1520) was an Italian Renaissance painter and architect, known for graceful, harmonious works like **The School of Athens** in the Vatican, which shows ancient philosophers including Plato and Aristotle. He died at just 37."],
  ["who was donatello | tell me about donatello the sculptor", "**Donatello** (c. 1386–1466) was an Italian sculptor from Florence and a pioneer of Renaissance art. His bronze **David** was the first free-standing nude statue made in Europe since ancient times. (Yes, the Ninja Turtles are named after him and other artists!)"],
  ["who was sandro botticelli | who was botticelli | tell me about botticelli", "**Sandro Botticelli** (c. 1445–1510) was an Italian Renaissance painter from Florence. He's famous for **The Birth of Venus**, showing the goddess arriving on a seashell, and **Primavera**."],
  ["who was rembrandt | tell me about rembrandt | rembrandt", "**Rembrandt van Rijn** (1606–1669) was a Dutch painter of the Golden Age, famous for his dramatic use of light and shadow. His best-known work is **The Night Watch**, and he painted dozens of honest self-portraits throughout his life."],
  ["who was johannes vermeer | who was vermeer", "**Johannes Vermeer** (1632–1675) was a Dutch painter known for calm scenes of everyday life filled with soft light. His most famous painting is **Girl with a Pearl Earring**. Only about 35 of his paintings are known today."],
  ["what is claude monet famous for | when did claude monet live", "**Claude Monet** (1840–1926) was a French painter famous for founding **Impressionism**; the movement was named after his painting *Impression, Sunrise* (1872). He painted the same scenes, like haystacks and his water lily pond, many times in different light."],
  ["who was pierre-auguste renoir | who was renoir | tell me about renoir", "**Pierre-Auguste Renoir** (1841–1919) was a French Impressionist painter known for joyful scenes of people, like **Luncheon of the Boating Party** and *Dance at Le Moulin de la Galette*. He kept painting into old age even with severe arthritis."],
  ["who was edgar degas | who was degas | tell me about degas", "**Edgar Degas** (1834–1917) was a French artist associated with Impressionism, famous for his paintings, pastels and sculptures of **ballet dancers**. His sculpture *Little Dancer Aged Fourteen* even wore a real tutu."],
  ["who was paul cezanne | who was cezanne | tell me about cezanne", "**Paul Cézanne** (1839–1906) was a French painter whose still lifes of apples and landscapes of Mont Sainte-Victoire built shapes out of blocks of color. He was a bridge between Impressionism and modern art; Picasso called him \"the father of us all.\""],
  ["what is van gogh famous for | when did van gogh live | did van gogh cut off his ear", "**Vincent van Gogh** (1853–1890) is famous for paintings like **The Starry Night** and **Sunflowers**, with bright colors and swirling brushstrokes. He made about 2,100 artworks in just over a decade. In 1888, during a mental health crisis, he cut off part of his own ear."],
  ["who was paul gauguin | who was gauguin | tell me about gauguin", "**Paul Gauguin** (1848–1903) was a French painter known for bold, flat colors. He spent his later years in **Tahiti** and other Pacific islands, where he painted many of his best-known works. He worked briefly alongside Van Gogh in 1888."],
  ["what is picasso famous for | when did picasso live | what is guernica", "**Pablo Picasso** (1881–1973) was a Spanish artist famous for co-founding **Cubism** with Georges Braque. His huge black-and-white painting **Guernica** (1937) shows the horrors of the bombing of a Spanish town. He created tens of thousands of artworks in his long life."],
  ["who was salvador dali | who was dali | tell me about salvador dali", "**Salvador Dalí** (1904–1989) was a Spanish **Surrealist** painter famous for dreamlike images. His best-known painting, **The Persistence of Memory** (1931), shows melting clocks, and he was known for his long, curled mustache."],
  ["who was henri matisse | who was matisse | tell me about matisse", "**Henri Matisse** (1869–1954) was a French artist famous for bright, bold colors and a style called **Fauvism**. Late in life, when he couldn't easily paint, he made colorful paper cut-outs he called \"painting with scissors.\""],
  ["what is frida kahlo famous for | when did frida kahlo live", "**Frida Kahlo** (1907–1954) is famous for her many vivid self-portraits, about 55 of them. After a serious bus accident at 18 left her in pain for life, she began painting in bed using a mirror. She was married to the muralist Diego Rivera."],
  ["who was diego rivera | tell me about diego rivera", "**Diego Rivera** (1886–1957) was a Mexican painter famous for huge **murals** showing workers, history and Mexican culture, painted on walls in Mexico City, Detroit and San Francisco. He was married to Frida Kahlo."],
  ["who was georgia o'keeffe | who was georgia okeeffe | tell me about georgia okeeffe", "**Georgia O'Keeffe** (1887–1986) was an American painter called the \"Mother of American modernism.\" She's famous for huge, close-up paintings of **flowers** and for desert landscapes and bones from New Mexico."],
  ["who was andy warhol | tell me about andy warhol", "**Andy Warhol** (1928–1987) was an American artist and leader of **Pop Art**. He's famous for paintings of everyday things like Campbell's Soup cans and colorful portraits of celebrities like Marilyn Monroe, and he said everyone would be \"famous for 15 minutes.\""],
  ["who was jackson pollock | tell me about jackson pollock", "**Jackson Pollock** (1912–1956) was an American painter and a leader of **Abstract Expressionism**. He laid huge canvases on the floor and dripped and poured paint onto them, a technique called \"drip painting.\""],
  ["who was hokusai | who was katsushika hokusai | who made the great wave", "**Katsushika Hokusai** (1760–1849) was a Japanese artist and printmaker. His woodblock print **The Great Wave off Kanagawa** (around 1831), showing a giant wave with Mount Fuji in the distance, is one of the most famous artworks in the world."],
  ["who was gustav klimt | who was klimt | tell me about klimt", "**Gustav Klimt** (1862–1918) was an Austrian painter known for decorative works covered in real **gold leaf**. His most famous painting is **The Kiss** (1907–1908) in Vienna."],
  ["who was edvard munch | tell me about edvard munch", "**Edvard Munch** (1863–1944) was a Norwegian painter best known for **The Scream** (1893), showing a figure holding its face under a swirling orange sky. He made several versions of it in paint and pastel."],
  ["who was rene magritte | who was magritte | tell me about magritte", "**René Magritte** (1898–1967) was a Belgian **Surrealist** painter known for witty, puzzling images, like a man whose face is hidden by a floating green apple (*The Son of Man*) and a pipe labeled \"This is not a pipe.\""],
  ["who was norman rockwell | tell me about norman rockwell", "**Norman Rockwell** (1894–1978) was an American illustrator famous for warm scenes of everyday American life, especially more than 300 covers for *The Saturday Evening Post*. His painting *The Problem We All Live With* shows Ruby Bridges walking to school."],
  ["who was auguste rodin | who was rodin | who made the thinker", "**Auguste Rodin** (1840–1917) was a French sculptor considered the father of modern sculpture. His most famous work is **The Thinker**, a bronze man sitting with his chin resting on his hand."],
  ["who was bob ross | tell me about bob ross | bob ross", "**Bob Ross** (1942–1995) was an American painter and host of the TV show *The Joy of Painting* (1983–1994). Known for his calm voice and big curly hair, he taught viewers to paint landscapes with \"happy little trees\" in about 30 minutes."],
  ["who was john james audubon | who was audubon | tell me about audubon", "**John James Audubon** (1785–1851) was an American naturalist and artist who painted life-size pictures of North American birds. His book *The Birds of America* has 435 colorful prints, and the National Audubon Society is named after him."],
  ["who was ansel adams | tell me about ansel adams", "**Ansel Adams** (1902–1984) was an American photographer famous for dramatic black-and-white photos of the American West, especially **Yosemite National Park**. He also campaigned to protect wilderness areas."],
  ["who was frank lloyd wright | tell me about frank lloyd wright", "**Frank Lloyd Wright** (1867–1959) was an American architect who designed buildings that blend with nature. His most famous is **Fallingwater** (1935), a house built over a waterfall in Pennsylvania; he also designed New York's spiral-shaped Guggenheim Museum."],
  ["who was antoni gaudi | who was gaudi", "**Antoni Gaudí** (1852–1926) was a Spanish architect from Barcelona famous for colorful, curvy, nature-inspired buildings. His masterpiece, the **Sagrada Família** basilica, was begun in 1882 and is still being finished today."],
  ["when did bach live | what is bach famous for | johann sebastian bach", "**Johann Sebastian Bach** (1685–1750) was a German Baroque composer and organist. He's famous for works like the **Brandenburg Concertos**, the *Toccata and Fugue in D minor* and the *St. Matthew Passion*, and he had 20 children, several of whom became composers too."],
  ["when did mozart live | what is mozart famous for | mozart facts", "**Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart** (1756–1791) was an Austrian composer who wrote his first pieces at age 5 and toured Europe as a child star. In just 35 years he wrote over 600 works, including the operas *The Magic Flute* and *The Marriage of Figaro*."],
  ["when did beethoven live | what is beethoven famous for | beethoven facts", "**Ludwig van Beethoven** (1770–1827) was a German composer famous for his **nine symphonies**, including the dramatic Fifth (\"da-da-da-DUM\") and the Ninth with its \"Ode to Joy.\" He kept composing even after he became completely deaf."],
  ["who was george frideric handel | who was handel | tell me about handel", "**George Frideric Handel** (1685–1759) was a German-born Baroque composer who spent most of his career in London. He's famous for the oratorio **Messiah**, with its \"Hallelujah\" chorus, and *Water Music*."],
  ["who was joseph haydn | who was haydn | tell me about haydn", "**Joseph Haydn** (1732–1809) was an Austrian composer called the \"father of the symphony\" and of the string quartet. He wrote over 100 symphonies, including the *Surprise Symphony*, which has a sudden loud chord to wake up sleepy listeners."],
  ["what is vivaldi famous for | when did vivaldi live", "**Antonio Vivaldi** (1678–1741) was an Italian Baroque composer and violinist famous for **The Four Seasons**, four violin concertos that paint musical pictures of spring, summer, autumn and winter. He was a priest nicknamed \"the Red Priest\" for his red hair."],
  ["who was franz schubert | who was schubert | tell me about schubert", "**Franz Schubert** (1797–1828) was an Austrian composer who wrote more than 600 songs, plus symphonies and piano music, before dying at just 31. His *Unfinished Symphony* and the song *Ave Maria* are especially famous."],
  ["who was johannes brahms | who was brahms | tell me about brahms", "**Johannes Brahms** (1833–1897) was a German Romantic composer and pianist. He wrote four symphonies and many piano pieces, but his most famous tune is the gentle **Brahms' Lullaby** (\"Lullaby and good night\")."],
  ["who was richard wagner | who was wagner | tell me about wagner", "**Richard Wagner** (1813–1883) was a German composer known for huge, dramatic operas. His four-opera cycle **The Ring of the Nibelung** takes about 15 hours to perform, and \"Ride of the Valkyries\" is one of its best-known pieces."],
  ["who was giuseppe verdi | who was verdi | tell me about verdi", "**Giuseppe Verdi** (1813–1901) was an Italian composer of operas, including **Aida**, **La Traviata** and **Rigoletto**. He's one of the most performed opera composers in the world."],
  ["what is chopin famous for | when did chopin live", "**Frédéric Chopin** (1810–1849) was a Polish composer and pianist who wrote almost entirely for piano. He's famous for his nocturnes, waltzes, études and polonaises, and he spent most of his adult life in Paris."],
  ["what is tchaikovsky famous for | when did tchaikovsky live", "**Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky** (1840–1893) is famous for the ballet scores **The Nutcracker**, **Swan Lake** and **Sleeping Beauty**, plus the *1812 Overture*. He was the first Russian composer to become famous worldwide."],
  ["who was claude debussy | who was debussy | tell me about debussy", "**Claude Debussy** (1862–1918) was a French composer whose dreamy, shimmering music is often linked to Impressionism. His piano piece **Clair de Lune** (\"Moonlight\") is one of the most loved pieces of classical music."],
  ["who was igor stravinsky | who was stravinsky | tell me about stravinsky", "**Igor Stravinsky** (1882–1971) was a Russian-born composer whose bold ballet **The Rite of Spring** caused a near riot at its 1913 premiere in Paris. He also wrote *The Firebird* and became one of the most influential composers of the 20th century."],
  ["who was george gershwin | who was gershwin | tell me about gershwin", "**George Gershwin** (1898–1937) was an American composer who blended jazz with classical music. He wrote **Rhapsody in Blue**, *An American in Paris*, and the opera *Porgy and Bess*, which includes the song \"Summertime.\""],
  ["who was scott joplin | tell me about scott joplin | who was the king of ragtime", "**Scott Joplin** (c. 1868–1917) was an African American composer and pianist known as the \"King of Ragtime.\" His best-known pieces are the **Maple Leaf Rag** and **The Entertainer**."],
  ["who was john philip sousa | tell me about john philip sousa", "**John Philip Sousa** (1854–1932) was an American composer and bandleader called \"The March King.\" He wrote **The Stars and Stripes Forever**, the official national march of the United States, and helped invent the sousaphone."],
  ["who was louis armstrong | tell me about louis armstrong | louis armstrong", "**Louis Armstrong** (1901–1971) was an American jazz trumpeter and singer from New Orleans, nicknamed \"Satchmo.\" He's one of the most influential musicians in jazz history and is known for his gravelly voice and the song **\"What a Wonderful World.\"**"],
  ["who was duke ellington | tell me about duke ellington", "**Duke Ellington** (1899–1974) was an American jazz composer, pianist and bandleader. He led his big band for about 50 years and wrote thousands of pieces, including \"It Don't Mean a Thing (If It Ain't Got That Swing)\" and \"Mood Indigo.\""],
  ["who was ella fitzgerald | tell me about ella fitzgerald", "**Ella Fitzgerald** (1917–1996) was an American jazz singer called the \"First Lady of Song.\" She was famous for her pure voice and her amazing **scat singing**, and she won 13 Grammy Awards."],
  ["who was billie holiday | tell me about billie holiday", "**Billie Holiday** (1915–1959) was an American jazz singer nicknamed \"Lady Day,\" known for her emotional, personal singing style. Her 1939 song **\"Strange Fruit\"** was a powerful protest against racism."],
  ["who was elvis presley | who was elvis | tell me about elvis", "**Elvis Presley** (1935–1977) was an American singer and actor known as the **\"King of Rock and Roll.\"** His hits include \"Hound Dog,\" \"Jailhouse Rock\" and \"Can't Help Falling in Love,\" and his Memphis home, Graceland, is now a museum."],
  ["who were the beatles | tell me about the beatles | the beatles", "**The Beatles** were a rock band from Liverpool, England: **John Lennon**, **Paul McCartney**, **George Harrison** and **Ringo Starr**. Active from 1960 to 1970, they're the best-selling music act of all time, with songs like \"Hey Jude,\" \"Yesterday\" and \"Let It Be.\""],
  ["who was john lennon | tell me about john lennon", "**John Lennon** (1940–1980) was an English singer and songwriter who co-founded **The Beatles** and wrote many of their hits with Paul McCartney. His solo song **\"Imagine\"** (1971) is a famous call for peace."],
  ["who was michael jackson | tell me about michael jackson", "**Michael Jackson** (1958–2009) was an American singer and dancer called the **\"King of Pop.\"** His 1982 album **Thriller** is the best-selling album of all time, and he popularized the **moonwalk** dance move."],
  ["who was bob marley | tell me about bob marley", "**Bob Marley** (1945–1981) was a Jamaican singer and songwriter who brought **reggae** music to the world. His songs, like \"One Love,\" \"Three Little Birds\" and \"Redemption Song,\" carry messages of peace, unity and hope."],
  ["who was johnny cash | tell me about johnny cash", "**Johnny Cash** (1932–2003) was an American country music singer known as \"The Man in Black.\" His deep voice and songs like \"I Walk the Line\" and \"Ring of Fire\" made him one of the best-selling musicians ever."],
  ["who was frank sinatra | tell me about frank sinatra", "**Frank Sinatra** (1915–1998) was an American singer and actor nicknamed \"Ol' Blue Eyes.\" He's one of the most popular singers of the 20th century, known for songs like \"Fly Me to the Moon,\" \"New York, New York\" and \"My Way.\""],
  ["who was freddie mercury | tell me about freddie mercury", "**Freddie Mercury** (1946–1991) was a British singer, born in Zanzibar, who fronted the rock band **Queen**. Famous for his powerful voice and showmanship, he wrote \"Bohemian Rhapsody\" and \"We Are the Champions.\""],
  ["who was ray charles | tell me about ray charles", "**Ray Charles** (1930–2004) was an American singer and pianist who became blind as a child. He helped create **soul music** by mixing gospel, blues and jazz, with hits like \"Georgia on My Mind\" and \"Hit the Road Jack.\""],
  ["who was aretha franklin | tell me about aretha franklin", "**Aretha Franklin** (1942–2018) was an American singer called the **\"Queen of Soul.\"** Her powerful voice made hits of \"Respect\" and \"Natural Woman,\" and in 1987 she became the first woman inducted into the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame."],
  ["who was whitney houston | tell me about whitney houston", "**Whitney Houston** (1963–2012) was an American singer famous for her powerful voice. Her version of **\"I Will Always Love You\"** is one of the best-selling singles of all time, and she starred in movies like *The Bodyguard*."],
  ["who was charlie chaplin | tell me about charlie chaplin | charlie chaplin", "**Charlie Chaplin** (1889–1977) was an English comic actor and filmmaker who became a star of **silent movies**. He's best remembered as \"The Tramp,\" a lovable character with a bowler hat, cane, tiny mustache and baggy pants."],
  ["what is charlie chaplin famous for | charlie chaplin movies", "**Charlie Chaplin** is famous for silent comedy films like **The Kid** (1921), **The Gold Rush** (1925), **City Lights** (1931) and **Modern Times** (1936). He wrote, directed, starred in and even composed music for many of his films."],
  ["who was walt disney | tell me about walt disney | walt disney", "**Walt Disney** (1901–1966) was an American animator and businessman who co-founded the Walt Disney Company with his brother Roy. He created **Mickey Mouse** with Ub Iwerks in 1928 and was the original voice of Mickey."],
  ["what is walt disney famous for | what did walt disney create | first disney movie", "**Walt Disney** is famous for *Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs* (1937), the first full-length cel-animated feature film, and for opening **Disneyland** in California in 1955. He won 22 Academy Awards, more than anyone else in history."],
  ["who was buster keaton | tell me about buster keaton", "**Buster Keaton** (1895–1966) was an American silent-film comedian nicknamed \"The Great Stone Face\" because he never smiled on screen. He did his own amazing stunts in films like **The General** (1926)."],
  ["who were laurel and hardy | tell me about laurel and hardy", "**Laurel and Hardy** were a comedy duo: thin Englishman **Stan Laurel** (1890–1965) and larger American **Oliver Hardy** (1892–1957). They made over 100 films together from the 1920s to the 1950s, full of slapstick mishaps."],
  ["who was harry houdini | who was houdini | tell me about houdini", "**Harry Houdini** (1874–1926) was a Hungarian-born American magician and the world's most famous **escape artist**. He escaped from handcuffs, straitjackets and locked water tanks, and later worked to expose fake psychics."],
  ["who was marilyn monroe | tell me about marilyn monroe", "**Marilyn Monroe** (1926–1962) was an American actress and singer who became one of Hollywood's biggest stars in the 1950s, in films like *Gentlemen Prefer Blondes* and *Some Like It Hot*. She remains a pop-culture icon."],
  ["who was audrey hepburn | tell me about audrey hepburn", "**Audrey Hepburn** (1929–1993) was a British actress and humanitarian, famous for films like *Roman Holiday* and *Breakfast at Tiffany's*. In later life she worked tirelessly as a **UNICEF** Goodwill Ambassador helping children."],
  ["who was judy garland | tell me about judy garland", "**Judy Garland** (1922–1969) was an American actress and singer best known for playing **Dorothy** in *The Wizard of Oz* (1939), where she sang \"Over the Rainbow.\""],
  ["who was shirley temple | tell me about shirley temple", "**Shirley Temple** (1928–2014) was the most famous child movie star of the 1930s, known for her curly hair, singing and tap dancing. As an adult she became a U.S. ambassador. A non-alcoholic drink is named after her."],
  ["who was fred astaire | tell me about fred astaire", "**Fred Astaire** (1899–1987) was an American dancer, singer and actor, considered one of the greatest dancers in film history. He made ten famous musicals with dance partner **Ginger Rogers**, including *Top Hat* (1935)."],
  ["who was gene kelly | tell me about gene kelly", "**Gene Kelly** (1912–1996) was an American dancer, actor and director, famous for his athletic dancing style. His most famous scene is from **Singin' in the Rain** (1952), where he dances joyfully through a rainstorm."],
  ["who was lucille ball | tell me about lucille ball", "**Lucille Ball** (1911–1989) was an American comedian and actress who starred in the TV classic **I Love Lucy** (1951–1957). She was also the first woman to run a major TV studio, Desilu, which produced the original *Star Trek*."],
  ["who was alfred hitchcock | tell me about alfred hitchcock", "**Alfred Hitchcock** (1899–1980) was an English film director called the \"Master of Suspense.\" He made more than 50 films, including *Psycho*, *Vertigo* and *The Birds*, and often made a quick cameo appearance in his own movies."],
  ["who was jim henson | tell me about jim henson | who created the muppets", "**Jim Henson** (1936–1990) was an American puppeteer who created **the Muppets**, including Kermit the Frog, whom he also performed. He helped create characters for *Sesame Street* and made films like *The Dark Crystal* and *Labyrinth*."],
  ["who was fred rogers | who was mister rogers | tell me about mr rogers", "**Fred Rogers** (1928–2003) was an American TV host who created and hosted **Mister Rogers' Neighborhood** (1968–2001). Wearing his famous cardigan sweaters, he gently talked with children about feelings, kindness and everyday life."],
  ["who was charles schulz | who created peanuts | who created charlie brown", "**Charles M. Schulz** (1922–2000) was an American cartoonist who created the comic strip **Peanuts**, starring Charlie Brown and his dog **Snoopy**. He drew it himself for nearly 50 years, about 17,800 strips."],
  ["who was stan lee | tell me about stan lee", "**Stan Lee** (1922–2018) was an American comic book writer and editor at **Marvel Comics**. With artists like Jack Kirby and Steve Ditko, he co-created Spider-Man, the X-Men, the Fantastic Four, Iron Man, Black Panther and the Hulk."],
  ["who was robin williams | tell me about robin williams", "**Robin Williams** (1951–2014) was an American comedian and actor known for his lightning-fast improvisation. He voiced the Genie in *Aladdin* (1992), starred in *Mrs. Doubtfire* and *Jumanji*, and won an Oscar for *Good Will Hunting*."],
  ["who was steve irwin | who was the crocodile hunter | tell me about steve irwin", "**Steve Irwin** (1962–2006) was an Australian wildlife expert and TV host known as **\"The Crocodile Hunter.\"** His enthusiasm (and his catchphrase \"Crikey!\") inspired millions to care about animals. He ran Australia Zoo with his family."],
  ["who was bruce lee | tell me about bruce lee", "**Bruce Lee** (1940–1973) was a Hong Kong-American martial artist and actor who made kung fu movies popular worldwide, in films like *Enter the Dragon*. He also created his own martial arts philosophy called **Jeet Kune Do**."],
  ["who was pt barnum | who was p t barnum | tell me about pt barnum", "**P. T. Barnum** (1810–1891) was an American showman famous for his traveling shows and for co-founding the **Barnum & Bailey Circus**, billed as \"The Greatest Show on Earth.\" The film *The Greatest Showman* is loosely based on his life."],
  ["who was akira kurosawa | tell me about akira kurosawa", "**Akira Kurosawa** (1910–1998) was a Japanese film director considered one of the greatest filmmakers ever. His samurai films like **Seven Samurai** (1954) influenced many later movies, including *Star Wars*."],
  ["who was osamu tezuka | who created astro boy", "**Osamu Tezuka** (1928–1989) was a Japanese cartoonist known as the \"God of Manga.\" He created **Astro Boy** and *Kimba the White Lion*, and his big-eyed drawing style shaped modern manga and anime."],
  ["who was jesse owens | tell me about jesse owens | jesse owens", "**Jesse Owens** (1913–1980) was an African American track and field athlete. At the **1936 Berlin Olympics** he won four gold medals, in the 100 m, 200 m, long jump and 4x100 m relay."],
  ["what is jesse owens famous for | why was jesse owens important", "**Jesse Owens** is famous for winning four gold medals at the 1936 Olympics in Nazi Germany, which challenged Hitler's false claims of racial superiority. A year earlier he set three world records and tied a fourth in about 45 minutes at a single college meet."],
  ["who was babe ruth | tell me about babe ruth | babe ruth", "**Babe Ruth** (1895–1948) was an American baseball player, nicknamed \"the Bambino\" and \"the Sultan of Swat.\" He played mostly for the **New York Yankees** and was one of the first five players elected to the Baseball Hall of Fame."],
  ["what is babe ruth famous for | how many home runs did babe ruth hit", "**Babe Ruth** is famous for his home runs: he hit **714** in his career and **60 in the 1927 season**, both records that stood for decades. He started out as a star pitcher before becoming baseball's greatest slugger."],
  ["who was pele | tell me about pele | pele", "**Pelé** (1940–2022), born Edson Arantes do Nascimento, was a Brazilian soccer player widely considered one of the greatest of all time. He played most of his career for the club **Santos** and later for the New York Cosmos."],
  ["what is pele famous for | how many world cups did pele win", "**Pelé** is famous for winning **three FIFA World Cups** with Brazil (1958, 1962 and 1970), the only player to do so. He won his first at just 17 and scored well over 1,000 goals in his career, counting friendly matches."],
  ["who was muhammad ali | tell me about muhammad ali | muhammad ali", "**Muhammad Ali** (1942–2016), born Cassius Clay, was an American boxer and one of the most famous athletes of the 20th century. He won an Olympic gold medal in 1960 and was a three-time world heavyweight champion."],
  ["what is muhammad ali famous for | float like a butterfly sting like a bee", "**Muhammad Ali** is famous for his boxing skill, his fast talk and the line **\"Float like a butterfly, sting like a bee.\"** He also took a stand on his beliefs by refusing to be drafted during the Vietnam War, for which he was stripped of his title, and later won it back."],
  ["who was jackie robinson | tell me about jackie robinson | jackie robinson", "**Jackie Robinson** (1919–1972) was an American baseball player who broke Major League Baseball's color barrier when he joined the **Brooklyn Dodgers** in 1947. His number, **42**, is retired across all of baseball, and every player wears it on Jackie Robinson Day."],
  ["who was jim thorpe | tell me about jim thorpe", "**Jim Thorpe** (1887–1953) was a Native American (Sac and Fox) athlete who won Olympic gold in the **pentathlon and decathlon** in 1912. He also played professional football, baseball and basketball, and is often called one of the greatest all-around athletes ever."],
  ["who was lou gehrig | tell me about lou gehrig", "**Lou Gehrig** (1903–1941) was a New York Yankees baseball star nicknamed \"the Iron Horse\" for playing 2,130 games in a row. He had to retire because of ALS, a disease now often called **Lou Gehrig's disease**, and gave a famous farewell speech calling himself \"the luckiest man on the face of the earth.\""],
  ["who was babe didrikson zaharias | who was babe didrikson | tell me about babe didrikson", "**Babe Didrikson Zaharias** (1911–1956) was an American athlete who won two gold medals in track and field at the 1932 Olympics and then became a champion golfer, winning 10 major golf championships."],
  ["who was wilma rudolph | tell me about wilma rudolph", "**Wilma Rudolph** (1940–1994) was an American sprinter who had polio as a child and was told she might never walk normally. At the 1960 Rome Olympics she became the first American woman to win **three gold medals** in track and field at one Games."],
  ["who was althea gibson | tell me about althea gibson", "**Althea Gibson** (1927–2003) was an American tennis player who in 1956 became the first Black player to win a Grand Slam title, the French Championships. She went on to win Wimbledon and the U.S. Nationals in 1957 and 1958."],
  ["who was arthur ashe | tell me about arthur ashe", "**Arthur Ashe** (1943–1993) was an American tennis player and the only Black man to win the singles titles at **Wimbledon**, the U.S. Open and the Australian Open. He was also a respected activist for civil rights and health education. The main stadium at the U.S. Open is named after him."],
  ["who was roberto clemente | tell me about roberto clemente", "**Roberto Clemente** (1934–1972) was a Puerto Rican baseball star for the Pittsburgh Pirates who got exactly 3,000 hits. He died in a plane crash while delivering aid to earthquake victims in Nicaragua, and baseball's award for community service is named after him."],
  ["who was joe louis | tell me about joe louis", "**Joe Louis** (1914–1981) was an American boxer nicknamed \"the Brown Bomber.\" He was world heavyweight champion from 1937 to 1949, one of the longest reigns in boxing history."],
  ["who was hank aaron | tell me about hank aaron", "**Hank Aaron** (1934–2021) was an American baseball player who broke Babe Ruth's career home run record in 1974, despite facing racist threats. He finished with **755 home runs**, the record for 33 years."],
  ["who was bill russell | tell me about bill russell", "**Bill Russell** (1934–2022) was an American basketball player who won **11 NBA championships** with the Boston Celtics, more than any other player. He later became the first Black head coach in the NBA, and the NBA Finals MVP award is named after him."],
  ["who was wilt chamberlain | tell me about wilt chamberlain", "**Wilt Chamberlain** (1936–1999) was an American basketball player who once scored **100 points** in a single NBA game, in 1962, a record that still stands. He stood about 7 feet 1 inch (2.16 m) tall."],
  ["who was ayrton senna | tell me about ayrton senna", "**Ayrton Senna** (1960–1994) was a Brazilian racing driver who won the **Formula One** World Championship three times (1988, 1990 and 1991). He's considered one of the fastest drivers ever, especially in the rain."],
  ["who was diego maradona | who was maradona | tell me about maradona", "**Diego Maradona** (1960–2020) was an Argentine soccer player who led Argentina to win the **1986 World Cup**. In that tournament he scored both the controversial \"Hand of God\" goal and a dazzling solo run voted the \"Goal of the Century.\""],
  ["who was johan cruyff | tell me about johan cruyff", "**Johan Cruyff** (1947–2016) was a Dutch soccer player and coach famous for \"Total Football,\" where players swap positions freely. A move he made famous, the **Cruyff turn**, is still taught today."],
  ["who was kobe bryant | tell me about kobe bryant", "**Kobe Bryant** (1978–2020) was an American basketball player who spent his entire 20-year NBA career with the **Los Angeles Lakers**, winning five championships. He scored 81 points in one game in 2006 and also won an Oscar for the short film *Dear Basketball*."],
  ["who was homer | tell me about homer the poet | homer greek poet", "**Homer** was an ancient Greek poet who probably lived around the 8th century BC. Very little is known about his life, and historians even debate whether he was one person or a tradition of storytellers."],
  ["what did homer write | what is the iliad | what is the odyssey", "**Homer** is credited with two great epic poems: the **Iliad**, about the Trojan War and the hero Achilles, and the **Odyssey**, about Odysseus's ten-year journey home after the war. They're among the oldest works of Western literature."],
  ["who was aesop | tell me about aesop | aesops fables", "**Aesop** (c. 620–564 BC) was an ancient Greek storyteller, possibly enslaved, credited with hundreds of short **fables** with morals, like *The Tortoise and the Hare*, *The Boy Who Cried Wolf* and *The Ant and the Grasshopper*."],
  ["who was virgil | tell me about virgil | what is the aeneid", "**Virgil** (70–19 BC) was the greatest poet of ancient Rome. His epic poem the **Aeneid** tells how the Trojan hero Aeneas traveled to Italy and became an ancestor of the Romans."],
  ["who was dante | who was dante alighieri | tell me about dante", "**Dante Alighieri** (c. 1265–1321) was an Italian poet from Florence. He's often called the \"father of the Italian language\" because he wrote in everyday Italian instead of Latin."],
  ["what did dante write | what is the divine comedy | dante's inferno", "**Dante** wrote the **Divine Comedy**, a long poem describing an imaginary journey through **Hell** (*Inferno*), **Purgatory** and **Heaven** (*Paradiso*), guided first by the poet Virgil. It's considered one of the greatest works of world literature."],
  ["who was geoffrey chaucer | who was chaucer | what are the canterbury tales", "**Geoffrey Chaucer** (c. 1343–1400) was an English poet called the \"father of English literature.\" He's famous for **The Canterbury Tales**, a collection of stories told by pilgrims traveling to Canterbury, written in Middle English."],
  ["who was murasaki shikibu | who wrote the tale of genji | first novel ever written", "**Murasaki Shikibu** (c. 973–c. 1014) was a Japanese writer and lady of the imperial court. Around 1010 she wrote **The Tale of Genji**, which is often called the world's first novel."],
  ["what is shakespeare famous for | when did shakespeare live | how many plays did shakespeare write", "**William Shakespeare** (1564–1616) was an English playwright and poet who wrote about **39 plays** and 154 sonnets. His plays include *Romeo and Juliet*, *Hamlet*, *Macbeth* and *A Midsummer Night's Dream*, and he invented or popularized many English words and phrases."],
  ["who was miguel de cervantes | who was cervantes | who wrote don quixote", "**Miguel de Cervantes** (1547–1616) was a Spanish writer who wrote **Don Quixote**, about a man who reads so many tales of knights that he sets off to become one, famously attacking windmills he thinks are giants. It's often called the first modern novel."],
  ["who was john milton | tell me about john milton | what is paradise lost", "**John Milton** (1608–1674) was an English poet best known for **Paradise Lost** (1667), an epic poem about the story of Adam and Eve. He wrote it after he had gone completely blind, dictating it line by line."],
  ["who was jane austen | tell me about jane austen | jane austen", "**Jane Austen** (1775–1817) was an English novelist known for witty stories about love, family and social class in the English countryside. Her novels were published anonymously during her lifetime."],
  ["what did jane austen write | jane austen books | who wrote pride and prejudice", "**Jane Austen** wrote six major novels: **Pride and Prejudice**, *Sense and Sensibility*, *Emma*, *Mansfield Park*, *Northanger Abbey* and *Persuasion*. *Pride and Prejudice* (1813), featuring Elizabeth Bennet and Mr. Darcy, is the most famous."],
  ["who was mary shelley | tell me about mary shelley | who wrote frankenstein", "**Mary Shelley** (1797–1851) was an English writer who wrote **Frankenstein** (1818) when she was only about 20. It tells of scientist Victor Frankenstein, who creates a living being, and is often called the first science fiction novel."],
  ["who were the bronte sisters | who was charlotte bronte | who was emily bronte", "The **Brontë sisters** were English writers: **Charlotte** (1816–1855) wrote *Jane Eyre*, **Emily** (1818–1848) wrote *Wuthering Heights*, and **Anne** (1820–1849) wrote *Agnes Grey*. They first published under male pen names."],
  ["who was charles dickens | tell me about charles dickens | charles dickens", "**Charles Dickens** (1812–1870) was an English novelist and the most popular writer of the Victorian era. As a boy he had to work in a factory, and his books often showed the hardships of poor people."],
  ["what did charles dickens write | charles dickens books | who wrote a christmas carol", "**Charles Dickens** wrote **A Christmas Carol** (1843), about the miser Ebenezer Scrooge, as well as *Oliver Twist*, *Great Expectations*, *David Copperfield* and *A Tale of Two Cities*. He invented the word \"Scrooge\" for a stingy person."],
  ["who was victor hugo | tell me about victor hugo | who wrote les miserables", "**Victor Hugo** (1802–1885) was a French writer famous for the novels **Les Misérables** and **The Hunchback of Notre-Dame**. He also wrote poetry and plays and campaigned against injustice and poverty."],
  ["who was leo tolstoy | who was tolstoy | who wrote war and peace", "**Leo Tolstoy** (1828–1910) was a Russian writer famous for **War and Peace**, an enormous novel set during Napoleon's invasion of Russia, and **Anna Karenina**. In later life he promoted nonviolence, which influenced Gandhi."],
  ["who was fyodor dostoevsky | who was dostoevsky | tell me about dostoevsky", "**Fyodor Dostoevsky** (1821–1881) was a Russian novelist who explored deep questions of right and wrong. His best-known books are **Crime and Punishment** and **The Brothers Karamazov**."],
  ["who was mark twain | tell me about mark twain | mark twain", "**Mark Twain** (1835–1910) was the pen name of **Samuel Clemens**, an American writer and humorist. He grew up in Missouri and worked as a riverboat pilot on the Mississippi; \"mark twain\" was a riverboat call meaning water two fathoms deep."],
  ["what did mark twain write | who wrote tom sawyer | who wrote huckleberry finn", "**Mark Twain** wrote **The Adventures of Tom Sawyer** (1876) and **Adventures of Huckleberry Finn** (1884), set along the Mississippi River. He also wrote *The Prince and the Pauper*, and was famous for his witty sayings."],
  ["who was edgar allan poe | who was poe | tell me about edgar allan poe", "**Edgar Allan Poe** (1809–1849) was an American writer of spooky poems and stories, like **The Raven** (\"Quoth the Raven, 'Nevermore'\") and *The Tell-Tale Heart*. He is also credited with inventing the detective story."],
  ["who was emily dickinson | tell me about emily dickinson", "**Emily Dickinson** (1830–1886) was an American poet who lived quietly in Amherst, Massachusetts, and wrote nearly 1,800 short poems, very few published in her lifetime. Lines like \"Hope is the thing with feathers\" made her one of America's most beloved poets."],
  ["who was walt whitman | tell me about walt whitman", "**Walt Whitman** (1819–1892) was an American poet famous for **Leaves of Grass**, a collection he kept expanding all his life. His free, unrhymed style celebrated ordinary people and democracy, and he wrote \"O Captain! My Captain!\" about Abraham Lincoln."],
  ["who was jules verne | tell me about jules verne", "**Jules Verne** (1828–1905) was a French writer and a founder of science fiction. His adventure novels include **Twenty Thousand Leagues Under the Seas**, **Around the World in Eighty Days** and *Journey to the Center of the Earth*."],
  ["who was h g wells | who was hg wells | tell me about hg wells", "**H. G. Wells** (1866–1946) was an English writer and a pioneer of science fiction. He wrote **The Time Machine**, **The War of the Worlds** (about a Martian invasion) and *The Invisible Man*."],
  ["who was lewis carroll | who wrote alice in wonderland | tell me about lewis carroll", "**Lewis Carroll** (1832–1898) was the pen name of **Charles Dodgson**, an English writer and Oxford mathematician. He wrote **Alice's Adventures in Wonderland** (1865) and *Through the Looking-Glass*, full of nonsense, riddles and characters like the Mad Hatter."],
  ["who was hans christian andersen | tell me about hans christian andersen", "**Hans Christian Andersen** (1805–1875) was a Danish author of fairy tales loved worldwide, including **The Little Mermaid**, **The Ugly Duckling**, *The Emperor's New Clothes* and *The Snow Queen*."],
  ["who were the brothers grimm | tell me about the brothers grimm | grimm fairy tales", "The **Brothers Grimm**, **Jacob** (1785–1863) and **Wilhelm** (1786–1859), were German scholars who collected folk tales. Their collection, first published in 1812, includes *Cinderella*, *Snow White*, *Hansel and Gretel* and *Rapunzel*."],
  ["who was robert louis stevenson | tell me about robert louis stevenson | who wrote treasure island", "**Robert Louis Stevenson** (1850–1894) was a Scottish writer famous for the pirate adventure **Treasure Island** (1883), with Long John Silver, and *Strange Case of Dr Jekyll and Mr Hyde*."],
  ["who was arthur conan doyle | who created sherlock holmes | tell me about arthur conan doyle", "**Sir Arthur Conan Doyle** (1859–1930) was a Scottish doctor and writer who created the detective **Sherlock Holmes** and his friend Dr. Watson, first appearing in *A Study in Scarlet* (1887)."],
  ["who was agatha christie | tell me about agatha christie", "**Agatha Christie** (1890–1976) was an English mystery writer, called the \"Queen of Crime.\" She created detectives **Hercule Poirot** and **Miss Marple**, and is one of the best-selling novelists of all time, with books like *Murder on the Orient Express*."],
  ["who was jrr tolkien | who was tolkien | tell me about tolkien", "**J. R. R. Tolkien** (1892–1973) was an English writer and Oxford professor of language. He invented whole languages for elves, and created the world of **Middle-earth**."],
  ["what did tolkien write | who wrote the hobbit | who wrote the lord of the rings", "**J. R. R. Tolkien** wrote **The Hobbit** (1937), about Bilbo Baggins, and **The Lord of the Rings** (1954–1955), the epic quest to destroy the One Ring. They're among the best-selling books ever written."],
  ["who was cs lewis | who was c s lewis | who wrote narnia", "**C. S. Lewis** (1898–1963) was a British writer and Oxford scholar, and a close friend of Tolkien. He wrote **The Chronicles of Narnia**, seven fantasy books beginning with *The Lion, the Witch and the Wardrobe* (1950)."],
  ["who was roald dahl | tell me about roald dahl | roald dahl books", "**Roald Dahl** (1916–1990) was a British children's author and former fighter pilot. He wrote **Charlie and the Chocolate Factory**, *Matilda*, *The BFG*, *James and the Giant Peach* and *The Witches*."],
  ["who was dr seuss | tell me about dr seuss | dr seuss books", "**Dr. Seuss** (1904–1991) was the pen name of **Theodor Geisel**, an American children's author and illustrator. He wrote over 60 books with playful rhymes, like **The Cat in the Hat**, *Green Eggs and Ham* and *How the Grinch Stole Christmas!*"],
  ["who was beatrix potter | who wrote peter rabbit | tell me about beatrix potter", "**Beatrix Potter** (1866–1943) was an English author, illustrator and naturalist who wrote **The Tale of Peter Rabbit** (1902) and about 22 more little books about animals. She later used her earnings to protect land in England's Lake District."],
  ["who was louisa may alcott | who wrote little women", "**Louisa May Alcott** (1832–1888) was an American author best known for **Little Women** (1868), the story of the four March sisters, Meg, Jo, Beth and Amy, growing up during the Civil War."],
  ["who was laura ingalls wilder | who wrote little house on the prairie", "**Laura Ingalls Wilder** (1867–1957) was an American author who wrote the **Little House** books about her childhood as a pioneer girl on the American frontier. She began publishing them in her 60s."],
  ["who was lm montgomery | who wrote anne of green gables", "**L. M. Montgomery** (1874–1942) was a Canadian author who wrote **Anne of Green Gables** (1908), about a talkative, red-haired orphan girl on Prince Edward Island."],
  ["who was george orwell | tell me about george orwell", "**George Orwell** (1903–1950) was the pen name of English writer Eric Blair. He's famous for **Animal Farm**, where farm animals take over a farm, and **1984**, about a government that watches everyone (\"Big Brother is watching you\")."],
  ["who was ernest hemingway | who was hemingway | tell me about hemingway", "**Ernest Hemingway** (1899–1961) was an American writer known for his short, simple sentences. He wrote *The Sun Also Rises*, *A Farewell to Arms* and **The Old Man and the Sea**, and won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1954."],
  ["who was f scott fitzgerald | who wrote the great gatsby", "**F. Scott Fitzgerald** (1896–1940) was an American writer who captured the glamorous \"Jazz Age\" of the 1920s. His most famous novel is **The Great Gatsby** (1925)."],
  ["who was harper lee | who wrote to kill a mockingbird", "**Harper Lee** (1926–2016) was an American writer from Alabama whose novel **To Kill a Mockingbird** (1960) tells of a lawyer, Atticus Finch, defending a Black man falsely accused of a crime. It won the Pulitzer Prize."],
  ["who was maya angelou | tell me about maya angelou", "**Maya Angelou** (1928–2014) was an American poet, author and civil rights activist. Her memoir **I Know Why the Caged Bird Sings** (1969) and her poem \"Still I Rise\" are celebrated around the world."],
  ["who was langston hughes | tell me about langston hughes", "**Langston Hughes** (1901–1967) was an African American poet and a leading voice of the **Harlem Renaissance** in the 1920s. Poems like \"The Negro Speaks of Rivers\" and \"Dreams\" celebrated Black life and hopes."],
  ["who was toni morrison | tell me about toni morrison", "**Toni Morrison** (1931–2019) was an American novelist who wrote powerful books about African American life, including *Beloved*, which won the Pulitzer Prize. In 1993 she became the first Black woman to win the Nobel Prize in Literature."],
  ["who was rabindranath tagore | who was tagore | tell me about tagore", "**Rabindranath Tagore** (1861–1941) was a Bengali poet, writer and composer from India. In 1913 he became the first non-European to win the Nobel Prize in Literature, and he wrote the national anthems of both India and Bangladesh."],
  ["who was goethe | who was johann wolfgang von goethe | tell me about goethe", "**Johann Wolfgang von Goethe** (1749–1832) was a German poet, playwright and scientist, often considered Germany's greatest writer. His most famous work is the play **Faust**, about a scholar who makes a deal with the devil."],
  ["who was oscar wilde | tell me about oscar wilde", "**Oscar Wilde** (1854–1900) was an Irish writer famous for his clever wit. He wrote the comedy **The Importance of Being Earnest**, the novel *The Picture of Dorian Gray*, and children's stories like *The Happy Prince*."],
  ["who was herman melville | who wrote moby dick", "**Herman Melville** (1819–1891) was an American writer best known for **Moby-Dick** (1851), the story of Captain Ahab's obsessive hunt for a giant white whale. Its first line is \"Call me Ishmael.\""],
  ["who was robert frost | tell me about robert frost", "**Robert Frost** (1874–1963) was an American poet known for poems about rural New England life, like **The Road Not Taken** and \"Stopping by Woods on a Snowy Evening.\" He won four Pulitzer Prizes."],
  ["who was shel silverstein | who wrote where the sidewalk ends", "**Shel Silverstein** (1930–1999) was an American poet and cartoonist famous for funny children's poetry books like **Where the Sidewalk Ends** and *A Light in the Attic*, and the picture book **The Giving Tree**."],
  ["who was eb white | who wrote charlottes web", "**E. B. White** (1899–1985) was an American writer who wrote the children's classics **Charlotte's Web** (1952), about a pig named Wilbur saved by a clever spider, and *Stuart Little*."],
  ["who wrote the little prince | who was antoine de saint-exupery", "**The Little Prince** (1943) was written by **Antoine de Saint-Exupéry** (1900–1944), a French writer and pilot. It's one of the most translated books in the world, famous for the line \"What is essential is invisible to the eye.\""],
  ["who was rumi | tell me about rumi", "**Rumi** (1207–1273) was a Persian poet and Sufi mystic. His poems about love, friendship and the search for meaning are still hugely popular, and his long poem the *Masnavi* is a classic of Persian literature."],
  ["who was li bai | who was li po | tell me about li bai", "**Li Bai** (701–762), also called Li Po, was one of the greatest poets of China's Tang dynasty. His poems about nature, the moon and friendship are still memorized by schoolchildren in China, such as \"Quiet Night Thought.\""],
  ["tell me about socrates | socrates", "**Socrates** (c. 470–399 BC) was a philosopher in ancient Athens and one of the founders of Western philosophy. He wrote nothing down himself; we know about him mostly through his student Plato."],
  ["what is socrates famous for | what is the socratic method | what did socrates teach", "**Socrates** is famous for the **Socratic method**: asking question after question to test ideas and uncover what people really know. He said \"the unexamined life is not worth living,\" and he was sentenced to death in 399 BC for supposedly corrupting the youth of Athens."],
  ["tell me about plato | plato", "**Plato** (c. 428–348 BC) was an ancient Greek philosopher, a student of Socrates and the teacher of Aristotle. He founded the **Academy** in Athens, one of the first schools of higher learning in the Western world."],
  ["what is plato famous for | what did plato write | what is the allegory of the cave", "**Plato** is famous for his dialogues, especially **The Republic**, about justice and the ideal society. In its **Allegory of the Cave**, prisoners who only see shadows on a wall mistake them for reality, a picture of how learning frees the mind."],
  ["tell me about aristotle | aristotle", "**Aristotle** (384–322 BC) was an ancient Greek philosopher and scientist, a student of Plato and the tutor of Alexander the Great. He founded his own school in Athens, the **Lyceum**."],
  ["what is aristotle famous for | what did aristotle teach", "**Aristotle** wrote about almost everything: logic, ethics, politics, poetry, biology and physics. He invented formal logic, classified hundreds of animals, and taught that a good life comes from developing virtues through practice."],
  ["what is confucius famous for | when did confucius live | confucius sayings", "**Confucius** (551–479 BC) was a Chinese teacher and philosopher whose sayings were collected by his followers in the **Analects**. He's famous for teaching respect for family, kindness, good government and a version of the Golden Rule: \"Do not do to others what you would not want done to yourself.\""],
  ["who was laozi | who was lao tzu | tell me about laozi", "**Laozi** (also spelled Lao Tzu) was an ancient Chinese philosopher, traditionally said to have lived in the 6th century BC, though some historians doubt he was a single real person. He is credited with founding **Taoism** (Daoism)."],
  ["what did laozi write | what is the tao te ching | what is taoism", "The **Tao Te Ching**, traditionally credited to **Laozi**, is a short book of poetic teachings about the **Tao** (\"the Way\"), the natural flow of the universe. Taoism encourages simplicity, humility and living in harmony with nature. A famous line: \"A journey of a thousand miles begins with a single step.\""],
  ["who was sun tzu | tell me about sun tzu | what is the art of war", "**Sun Tzu** was a Chinese military general and strategist traditionally said to have lived around the 5th century BC. He's credited with **The Art of War**, a book of strategy still read by leaders, coaches and businesspeople, which teaches that the best victory is one won without fighting."],
  ["who was marcus aurelius | tell me about marcus aurelius | what is meditations", "**Marcus Aurelius** (121–180 AD) was a Roman emperor and a **Stoic** philosopher. His private journal, later published as **Meditations**, gives advice on staying calm, doing your duty and focusing on what you can control."],
  ["who was diogenes | tell me about diogenes", "**Diogenes** (c. 404–323 BC) was a Greek philosopher who lived very simply, reportedly sleeping in a large clay jar in Athens. A famous story says that when Alexander the Great offered him anything he wanted, Diogenes asked him to step aside because he was blocking the sunlight."],
  ["who was epicurus | tell me about epicurus", "**Epicurus** (341–270 BC) was a Greek philosopher who taught that the goal of life is happiness, found through simple pleasures, friendship and freedom from fear, not luxury."],
  ["who was thomas aquinas | tell me about thomas aquinas", "**Thomas Aquinas** (1225–1274) was an Italian priest and philosopher who combined Aristotle's ideas with Christian teaching. His huge work, the *Summa Theologica*, made him one of the most influential thinkers of the Middle Ages."],
  ["who was niccolo machiavelli | who was machiavelli | what is the prince by machiavelli", "**Niccolò Machiavelli** (1469–1527) was an Italian diplomat and writer from Florence. His book **The Prince** (1532) gives blunt advice to rulers on gaining and keeping power, which is why \"Machiavellian\" now means cunning."],
  ["who was rene descartes | who was descartes | i think therefore i am", "**René Descartes** (1596–1650) was a French philosopher and mathematician, often called the father of modern philosophy. He's famous for the line **\"I think, therefore I am,\"** and invented the **Cartesian coordinate system** (x and y axes) used in graphs."],
  ["who was thomas hobbes | tell me about thomas hobbes", "**Thomas Hobbes** (1588–1679) was an English philosopher who wrote **Leviathan** (1651). He argued that without government, life would be \"nasty, brutish and short,\" so people agree to give some power to a ruler to keep peace."],
  ["who was john locke | tell me about john locke", "**John Locke** (1632–1704) was an English philosopher who argued that all people have natural rights to **life, liberty and property** and that governments should rule by the consent of the people. His ideas strongly influenced the U.S. Declaration of Independence."],
  ["who was voltaire | tell me about voltaire", "**Voltaire** (1694–1778) was the pen name of French writer François-Marie Arouet, a leading thinker of the **Enlightenment**. He championed freedom of speech and religious tolerance, and wrote the satire *Candide*."],
  ["who was jean-jacques rousseau | who was rousseau | tell me about rousseau", "**Jean-Jacques Rousseau** (1712–1778) was a Geneva-born philosopher of the Enlightenment. His book *The Social Contract* begins \"Man is born free, and everywhere he is in chains,\" and his ideas influenced the French Revolution and modern education."],
  ["who was immanuel kant | who was kant | tell me about kant", "**Immanuel Kant** (1724–1804) was a German philosopher who explored how we know things and how we should act. He argued we should act only in ways we'd want everyone to act, an idea called the **categorical imperative**. He famously took a walk at the same time every day."],
  ["who was adam smith | tell me about adam smith", "**Adam Smith** (1723–1790) was a Scottish philosopher and economist called the \"father of economics.\" His book **The Wealth of Nations** (1776) explained how free markets and the division of labor create wealth, guided by what he called an \"invisible hand.\""],
  ["who was karl marx | tell me about karl marx", "**Karl Marx** (1818–1883) was a German philosopher and economist who wrote *The Communist Manifesto* (1848) with Friedrich Engels and *Das Kapital*. His theories about class struggle and capitalism became the basis of communism and remain widely debated."],
  ["who was friedrich nietzsche | who was nietzsche | tell me about nietzsche", "**Friedrich Nietzsche** (1844–1900) was a German philosopher who questioned traditional morals and religion. He wrote *Thus Spoke Zarathustra* and is known for ideas like the \"will to power\" and the saying \"What doesn't kill me makes me stronger.\""],
  ["who was simone de beauvoir | tell me about simone de beauvoir", "**Simone de Beauvoir** (1908–1986) was a French philosopher and writer. Her book **The Second Sex** (1949) examined how women had been treated throughout history and became a foundational work of modern feminism."],
  ["who was the buddha | who was siddhartha gautama | tell me about buddha", "**Siddhartha Gautama**, known as the **Buddha** (\"the awakened one\"), was a spiritual teacher who lived in ancient India and Nepal around the 5th century BC. According to tradition, he was a prince who left his palace to seek an end to suffering and reached enlightenment while meditating under a tree."],
  ["what did the buddha teach | what is buddhism | what are the four noble truths", "**Buddhism** is based on the teachings of the Buddha. At its heart are the **Four Noble Truths**, which say life involves suffering, suffering comes from craving, it can end, and the way to end it is the **Eightfold Path** of wise living, kindness and meditation. Today it has around 500 million followers."],
  ["who was jesus | who was jesus christ | tell me about jesus", "**Jesus of Nazareth** (c. 4 BC–c. 30 AD) was a Jewish teacher and preacher in Roman-ruled Judea. Christians believe he is the Son of God and the Messiah; his life and teachings are described in the Gospels of the New Testament, and Christianity, the world's largest religion, is based on them."],
  ["what did jesus teach | teachings of jesus", "According to the Gospels, **Jesus** taught love of God and love of neighbor, forgiveness, and caring for the poor and outcast, often through stories called **parables**, like the Good Samaritan. His Sermon on the Mount includes the Beatitudes and the Golden Rule."],
  ["who was muhammad | who was the prophet muhammad | tell me about muhammad", "**Muhammad** (c. 570–632) was born in Mecca in Arabia and is regarded by Muslims as the final prophet of God. Muslims believe the **Quran** was revealed to him over about 23 years, and he founded the religion of **Islam**, which today has nearly 2 billion followers."],
  ["who was moses | tell me about moses", "**Moses** is a central figure in Judaism, Christianity and Islam. According to the Hebrew Bible, he led the Israelites out of slavery in Egypt and received the **Ten Commandments** from God on Mount Sinai. Historians debate how much of his story is historical."],
  ["who was abraham in the bible | who was abraham the patriarch", "**Abraham** is a figure honored in Judaism, Christianity and Islam, which are often called the \"Abrahamic religions.\" According to tradition, he was a man from Mesopotamia who made a covenant with God and became the ancestor of many nations."],
  ["who was guru nanak | tell me about guru nanak | who founded sikhism", "**Guru Nanak** (1469–1539) was the founder of **Sikhism**, born in what is now Pakistan. He taught belief in one God, the equality of all people, honest work and sharing with others. He was the first of the ten Sikh Gurus."],
  ["who was martin luther the monk", "**Martin Luther** (1483–1546) was a German monk and professor who in 1517 published his **95 Theses** criticizing practices of the Catholic Church. This launched the **Protestant Reformation**, and his German translation of the Bible helped shape the German language."],
  ["who was zoroaster | who was zarathustra | what is zoroastrianism", "**Zoroaster** (also called Zarathustra) was an ancient Persian prophet who founded **Zoroastrianism**, one of the world's oldest religions still practiced. Scholars disagree on when he lived, with estimates from about 1500 to 500 BC. He taught a struggle between good and evil and the motto \"good thoughts, good words, good deeds.\""],
  ["who was mahavira | tell me about mahavira | what is jainism", "**Mahavira** (traditionally 599–527 BC, though many scholars place him a little later) was an Indian spiritual teacher who is the most important figure in **Jainism**. Jainism teaches **ahimsa**, nonviolence toward all living things, as well as truthfulness and simple living."],
  ["who was saint patrick | who was st patrick | tell me about saint patrick", "**Saint Patrick** (5th century AD) was a Christian missionary in Ireland and is the country's patron saint. Born in Roman Britain, he was kidnapped as a teenager and enslaved in Ireland, escaped, and later returned to spread Christianity. St. Patrick's Day is celebrated on March 17."],
  ["who was saint francis of assisi | who was st francis | tell me about francis of assisi", "**Francis of Assisi** (c. 1181–1226) was an Italian friar who gave up his wealth to live in poverty and serve the poor. He founded the Franciscan order and loved nature and animals, so he's the patron saint of animals and ecology."],
  ["who was saint nicholas | was santa claus a real person", "The legend of Santa Claus is partly based on **Saint Nicholas** (c. 270–343), a real Christian bishop of Myra in what is now Turkey. He was known for his generosity and for secretly giving gifts, including, according to legend, dropping gold coins down a chimney."],
  ["who was the dalai lama | what is the dalai lama", "The **Dalai Lama** is the title of the spiritual leader of Tibetan Buddhism. Tibetans believe each Dalai Lama is a reincarnation of the one before. The current, 14th Dalai Lama, born in 1935, has lived in India since 1959 and won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1989."],
  ["who was pierre curie | tell me about pierre curie", "**Pierre Curie** (1859–1906) was a French physicist and the husband of Marie Curie. Together they discovered the elements **polonium** and **radium**, and he shared the 1903 Nobel Prize in Physics with Marie and Henri Becquerel."],
  ["who was lord kelvin | who was william thomson kelvin | what is the kelvin scale", "**Lord Kelvin** (William Thomson, 1824–1907) was a Scottish-Irish physicist who worked on heat and energy. The **Kelvin temperature scale** starts at absolute zero, the coldest possible temperature (−273.15 °C), and is named after him."],
  ["who was anders celsius | who invented celsius", "**Anders Celsius** (1701–1744) was a Swedish astronomer who created a temperature scale in 1742 with 100 steps between the freezing and boiling points of water. Oddly, his original scale was upside down, with 0 for boiling; it was flipped after his death."],
  ["who was daniel fahrenheit | who invented fahrenheit | who invented the mercury thermometer", "**Daniel Gabriel Fahrenheit** (1686–1736) was a German-born physicist who worked in the Netherlands. He invented the **mercury thermometer** in 1714 and the **Fahrenheit scale**, on which water freezes at 32° and boils at 212°."],
  ["who was james joule | tell me about james joule", "**James Prescott Joule** (1818–1889) was an English physicist and brewer who showed that heat is a form of energy and that energy is conserved. The unit of energy, the **joule**, is named after him."],
  ["who was alfred wegener | who came up with continental drift", "**Alfred Wegener** (1880–1930) was a German scientist who proposed in 1912 that the continents were once joined in a supercontinent called **Pangaea** and slowly drifted apart. He was mocked at first, but his idea led to the modern theory of plate tectonics."],
  ["who was galen | tell me about galen the physician", "**Galen** (129–c. 216 AD) was a Greek physician in the Roman Empire who treated gladiators and emperors. His writings on anatomy and medicine were followed by doctors for more than 1,300 years, although many of his ideas were later proven wrong."],
  ["who was andreas vesalius | who was vesalius | tell me about vesalius", "**Andreas Vesalius** (1514–1564) was a Flemish doctor who founded modern human anatomy. His 1543 book *On the Fabric of the Human Body* had detailed drawings based on real dissections and corrected many of Galen's mistakes."],
  ["who was william harvey | who discovered blood circulation", "**William Harvey** (1578–1657) was an English doctor who discovered in 1628 that the **heart pumps blood** around the body in a continuous circuit through arteries and veins. Before that, people believed blood was constantly made and used up."],
  ["who was ignaz semmelweis | who discovered handwashing", "**Ignaz Semmelweis** (1818–1865) was a Hungarian doctor who discovered in 1847 that doctors **washing their hands** with a chlorine solution dramatically cut deaths of new mothers. He was ignored in his time, but he's now called the \"savior of mothers.\""],
  ["who was mary seacole | tell me about mary seacole", "**Mary Seacole** (1805–1881) was a Jamaican-born British nurse and businesswoman. During the Crimean War she set up the \"British Hotel\" near the battlefront, where she cared for sick and wounded soldiers."],
  ["who was aryabhata | tell me about aryabhata", "**Aryabhata** (476–550) was an Indian mathematician and astronomer. He calculated π very accurately, explained that Earth spins on its axis, and correctly said that eclipses are caused by shadows. India's first satellite was named after him."],
  ["who was brahmagupta | tell me about brahmagupta", "**Brahmagupta** (c. 598–668) was an Indian mathematician and astronomer who was the first to write down clear rules for calculating with **zero** and negative numbers, in 628 AD."],
  ["who was omar khayyam | tell me about omar khayyam", "**Omar Khayyam** (1048–1131) was a Persian mathematician, astronomer and poet. He solved cubic equations, helped create a highly accurate calendar, and is famous in the West for the poems of the **Rubaiyat**."],
  ["who was zhang heng | who invented the seismometer", "**Zhang Heng** (78–139 AD) was a Chinese scientist and inventor of the Han dynasty. In 132 AD he built the first known **seismoscope**, a bronze jar with dragons that dropped a ball into a toad's mouth to show the direction of an earthquake."],
  ["who was cai lun | tell me about cai lun", "**Cai Lun** (c. 50–121 AD) was an official at the Chinese imperial court who is traditionally credited with inventing **paper** around 105 AD, using tree bark, hemp, rags and old fishing nets. Simpler kinds of paper existed earlier, but he greatly improved the process."],
  ["who was ibn khaldun | tell me about ibn khaldun", "**Ibn Khaldun** (1332–1406) was a North African historian and scholar born in Tunis. His book the *Muqaddimah* studied why societies rise and fall, and he's considered a founder of sociology and economics."],
  ["who was alexander von humboldt | who was humboldt | tell me about alexander von humboldt", "**Alexander von Humboldt** (1769–1859) was a German naturalist and explorer who traveled through South America from 1799 to 1804. He saw nature as one connected web of life, and more places and species are named after him than after almost anyone else, including the Humboldt penguin."],
  ["who was alfred russel wallace | tell me about alfred russel wallace", "**Alfred Russel Wallace** (1823–1913) was a British naturalist who came up with the theory of **evolution by natural selection** at the same time as Charles Darwin. Their ideas were presented together in 1858."],
  ["who was john muir | tell me about john muir", "**John Muir** (1838–1914) was a Scottish-American naturalist and writer called the \"Father of the National Parks.\" He helped protect **Yosemite** and other wild places, and co-founded the Sierra Club in 1892."],
  ["who was henry david thoreau | who was thoreau | what is walden", "**Henry David Thoreau** (1817–1862) was an American writer and naturalist. He lived simply in a cabin by **Walden Pond** for two years and wrote about it in *Walden*. His essay \"Civil Disobedience\" inspired Gandhi and Martin Luther King Jr."],
  ["who was george stephenson | who invented the steam train", "**George Stephenson** (1781–1848) was an English engineer called the \"Father of Railways.\" He built the first public railway line to use steam locomotives, opened in 1825, and with his son Robert built the famous locomotive *Rocket* in 1829."],
  ["who was isambard kingdom brunel | who was brunel | tell me about brunel", "**Isambard Kingdom Brunel** (1806–1859) was a British engineer who built the **Great Western Railway**, bridges like the Clifton Suspension Bridge, and the giant steamships *Great Western* and *Great Eastern*. He's known for his top hat and cigar."],
  ["who was eli whitney | who invented the cotton gin", "**Eli Whitney** (1765–1825) was an American inventor who patented the **cotton gin** in 1794, a machine that quickly separated cotton fibers from seeds. It made cotton very profitable but sadly also increased the demand for enslaved labor in the South."],
  ["who was benjamin banneker | tell me about benjamin banneker", "**Benjamin Banneker** (1731–1806) was a free African American scientist, mathematician and almanac author. He built a wooden clock that kept time for decades and helped survey the land for **Washington, D.C.**"],
  ["who was garrett morgan | who invented the traffic light", "**Garrett Morgan** (1877–1963) was an African American inventor who patented an improved **three-position traffic signal** in 1923 and a safety hood (an early gas mask) used to rescue trapped workers. Earlier traffic signals existed, but his design helped make intersections safer."],
  ["who was madam cj walker | who was madam c j walker | tell me about madam cj walker", "**Madam C. J. Walker** (1867–1919) was an African American businesswoman who built a successful company making hair-care products for Black women. She is often cited as the first female self-made millionaire in America and gave generously to charities."],
  ["who was granville woods | tell me about granville woods", "**Granville Woods** (1856–1910) was an African American inventor called the \"Black Edison.\" He held around 60 patents, including systems that let moving trains communicate with stations and improvements to electric railways."],
  ["who was lewis latimer | tell me about lewis latimer", "**Lewis Latimer** (1848–1928) was an African American inventor and draftsman. He drew the patent drawings for Alexander Graham Bell's telephone and invented an improved, longer-lasting **carbon filament** for light bulbs."],
  ["who was john logie baird | tell me about john logie baird", "**John Logie Baird** (1888–1946) was a Scottish engineer who gave the first public demonstration of **television** in London in 1926, using a mechanical spinning-disc system. He also showed the first color TV pictures in 1928."],
  ["who was philo farnsworth | tell me about philo farnsworth", "**Philo Farnsworth** (1906–1971) was an American inventor who came up with the idea for **electronic television** as a teenager on an Idaho farm. In 1927 he transmitted the first fully electronic TV image, a single straight line."],
  ["who was rudolf diesel | who invented the diesel engine", "**Rudolf Diesel** (1858–1913) was a German engineer who invented the **diesel engine** in the 1890s, which ignites fuel using the heat of highly compressed air instead of a spark plug. Diesel engines power most trucks, ships and trains today."],
  ["who was igor sikorsky | tell me about igor sikorsky", "**Igor Sikorsky** (1889–1972) was a Russian-American aviation pioneer who built the first practical single-rotor **helicopter**, the VS-300, which flew in 1939. He also designed early large airplanes and seaplanes."],
  ["who was wernher von braun | tell me about wernher von braun", "**Wernher von Braun** (1912–1977) was a German-born rocket engineer who developed rockets for Nazi Germany during World War II and then for the United States. He led the design of the **Saturn V** rocket that carried astronauts to the Moon."],
  ["who was sergei korolev | tell me about sergei korolev", "**Sergei Korolev** (1907–1966) was the chief designer of the Soviet space program. His team launched **Sputnik**, the first satellite, in 1957, and sent Yuri Gagarin into space in 1961. His identity was kept secret until after his death."],
  ["who was christa mcauliffe | tell me about christa mcauliffe", "**Christa McAuliffe** (1948–1986) was an American schoolteacher chosen to be the first teacher in space. She died with her six crewmates when the **Space Shuttle Challenger** broke apart shortly after launch on January 28, 1986."],
  ["what is charles lindbergh famous for | what was the spirit of st louis", "**Charles Lindbergh** (1902–1974) is famous for making the first solo nonstop flight across the Atlantic Ocean, from New York to Paris, in May 1927. His plane was called the **Spirit of St. Louis**, and the flight took about 33.5 hours."],
  ["what is sacagawea famous for | when did sacagawea live", "**Sacagawea** (c. 1788–1812) was a Lemhi Shoshone woman famous for helping the Lewis and Clark expedition (1804–1806) as an interpreter and guide, while carrying her baby son. She appears on the U.S. golden dollar coin."],
  ["what is marco polo the game named after | why is marco polo game called that", "Nobody knows for sure why the swimming-pool game is called **Marco Polo**. It's named after the Venetian explorer **Marco Polo** (1254–1324), possibly because, like an explorer, the person who is \"it\" must search without seeing."],
  ["who was levi strauss | who invented blue jeans", "**Levi Strauss** (1829–1902) was a German-born American businessman in San Francisco. In 1873 he and tailor **Jacob Davis** patented work pants strengthened with metal rivets, the first **blue jeans**."],
  ["who was elisha otis | who invented the safety elevator", "**Elisha Otis** (1811–1861) was an American inventor who created the **safety elevator** in 1852, with a brake that stopped it from falling if the cable broke. This made tall skyscrapers practical."],
  ["who was willis carrier | who invented air conditioning", "**Willis Carrier** (1876–1950) was an American engineer who invented modern **air conditioning** in 1902, originally to control humidity in a printing plant in New York."],
  ["who was percy spencer | who invented the microwave oven", "**Percy Spencer** (1894–1970) was an American engineer who noticed that radar equipment melted a candy bar in his pocket. This led him to invent the **microwave oven**, patented in 1945."],
  ["who was nicolaus otto | who invented the four stroke engine", "**Nicolaus Otto** (1832–1891) was a German engineer who built the first practical **four-stroke internal combustion engine** in 1876. Most car engines today still use his four-stroke cycle."],
  ["who was james naismith | who invented basketball", "**James Naismith** (1861–1939) was a Canadian-American physical education teacher who invented **basketball** in 1891 in Springfield, Massachusetts. The first game used peach baskets and a soccer ball."],
  ["who was pierre de coubertin | who started the modern olympics", "**Pierre de Coubertin** (1863–1937) was a French educator who founded the **International Olympic Committee** in 1894 and revived the Olympic Games. The first modern Olympics were held in Athens in 1896."],
  ["who was nefertiti | tell me about nefertiti", "**Nefertiti** (c. 1370–c. 1330 BC) was an Egyptian queen, the wife of Pharaoh Akhenaten. She's famous for her beautiful painted limestone **bust**, discovered in 1912 and now in a museum in Berlin."],
  ["who was boudica | who was boudicca | tell me about boudica", "**Boudica** (died c. 61 AD) was a queen of the Iceni tribe in Britain who led a major uprising against the Roman Empire around 60–61 AD. Her army burned several Roman towns, including London, before being defeated."],
  ["who was wu zetian | who was the only female emperor of china", "**Wu Zetian** (624–705) was the only woman to rule China as emperor in her own right, founding her own Zhou dynasty in 690. She expanded the empire and promoted officials chosen by exams rather than family connections."],
  ["who was spartacus | tell me about spartacus", "**Spartacus** (c. 111–71 BC) was a gladiator who escaped from a training school in Italy in 73 BC and led a huge revolt of enslaved people against Rome. His army of tens of thousands won several battles before being defeated in 71 BC."],
  ["who was pericles | tell me about pericles", "**Pericles** (c. 495–429 BC) was the leading statesman of ancient Athens during its golden age. He strengthened democracy and paid for the building of the **Parthenon** on the Acropolis."],
  ["who was leonidas | who was king leonidas | tell me about leonidas", "**Leonidas I** (died 480 BC) was a king of Sparta who led a small Greek force, including his famous 300 Spartans, against a huge Persian army at the **Battle of Thermopylae** in 480 BC. Their last stand became a legendary symbol of courage."],
  ["who was constantine the great | tell me about constantine the great", "**Constantine the Great** (c. 272–337) was a Roman emperor who became the first emperor to convert to Christianity and ended the persecution of Christians. He founded the city of **Constantinople** (now Istanbul) as the empire's new capital in 330."],
  ["who was alfred the great | tell me about alfred the great", "**Alfred the Great** (849–899) was a king of Wessex in England who defended his kingdom against Viking invasions. He promoted education and laws, and is the only English king called \"the Great.\" A legend says he once burned some cakes while lost in thought."],
  ["who was william wallace | tell me about william wallace", "**William Wallace** (c. 1270–1305) was a Scottish knight who led resistance against English rule. He won a famous victory at the **Battle of Stirling Bridge** in 1297. The movie *Braveheart* is loosely based on his life."],
  ["who was robert the bruce | tell me about robert the bruce", "**Robert the Bruce** (1274–1329) was king of Scotland who won Scottish independence from England after his victory at the **Battle of Bannockburn** in 1314. A legend says he was inspired to keep trying by watching a spider spin its web."],
  ["who was king arthur | was king arthur real", "**King Arthur** is a legendary British king who, in medieval stories, led the Knights of the Round Table from his castle at Camelot and pulled the sword from the stone. Historians don't know whether a real Arthur existed; he may be based on a war leader of around 500 AD."],
  ["who was robin hood | was robin hood real", "**Robin Hood** is a legendary English outlaw who, in stories going back to the 1300s, lived in Sherwood Forest with his Merry Men and \"robbed from the rich to give to the poor.\" There's no clear evidence he was a real person."],
  ["who were ferdinand and isabella | who was queen isabella | who paid for columbus voyage", "**Ferdinand II of Aragon** (1452–1516) and **Isabella I of Castile** (1451–1504) were the married monarchs who united Spain. In 1492 they completed the conquest of Granada and paid for Christopher Columbus's first voyage across the Atlantic."],
  ["who was lorenzo de medici | who were the medici", "**Lorenzo de' Medici** (1449–1492), called \"Lorenzo the Magnificent,\" was a ruler of Florence from the wealthy **Medici** banking family. He supported artists like Botticelli and the young Michelangelo, helping make Florence the heart of the Renaissance."],
  ["who was tokugawa ieyasu | tell me about tokugawa ieyasu", "**Tokugawa Ieyasu** (1543–1616) was a Japanese military leader who unified Japan and became **shogun** in 1603. His family ruled Japan for over 250 years, a long period of peace called the Edo period."],
  ["who was sun yat-sen | who was sun yat sen | tell me about sun yat sen", "**Sun Yat-sen** (1866–1925) was a Chinese revolutionary who helped overthrow the last imperial dynasty, the Qing, in 1911–1912. He became the first provisional president of the Republic of China and is honored as the \"Father of the Nation.\""],
  ["who was kamehameha | who was king kamehameha | tell me about kamehameha", "**Kamehameha I** (c. 1758–1819), also called Kamehameha the Great, united the Hawaiian Islands into a single kingdom in 1810. Kamehameha Day is still celebrated in Hawaii every June 11."],
  ["who was queen liliuokalani | who was the last queen of hawaii", "**Liliʻuokalani** (1838–1917) was the last monarch of the Kingdom of Hawaii, reigning from 1891 until she was overthrown in 1893. She was also a songwriter who composed the famous Hawaiian song \"Aloha ʻOe.\""],
  ["who was ida b wells | tell me about ida b wells", "**Ida B. Wells** (1862–1931) was an African American journalist and civil rights activist who bravely investigated and exposed lynching in the American South. She also helped found the NAACP and fought for women's right to vote."],
  ["who was mary mcleod bethune | tell me about mary mcleod bethune", "**Mary McLeod Bethune** (1875–1955) was an African American educator who started a school for Black girls in Florida in 1904 with just $1.50. It grew into Bethune-Cookman University, and she later advised President Franklin D. Roosevelt."],
  ["who was jane addams | tell me about jane addams", "**Jane Addams** (1860–1935) was an American social reformer who founded **Hull House** in Chicago in 1889, a center offering education and help to poor immigrant families. In 1931 she became the first American woman to win the Nobel Peace Prize."],
  ["who was juliette gordon low | who started the girl scouts", "**Juliette Gordon Low** (1860–1927) founded the **Girl Scouts** in the United States in 1912 in Savannah, Georgia. She was inspired by Robert Baden-Powell, who started the Boy Scouts in Britain."],
  ["who was robert baden-powell | who was baden powell | who started the boy scouts", "**Robert Baden-Powell** (1857–1941) was a British army officer who founded the **Scouting** movement in 1907–1908. His book *Scouting for Boys* inspired millions of young people around the world, and he later helped start the Girl Guides with his sister Agnes."],
  ["who was josephine baker | tell me about josephine baker", "**Josephine Baker** (1906–1975) was an American-born French entertainer who became a huge star in Paris in the 1920s. During World War II she helped the French Resistance, and later she spoke at the 1963 March on Washington."],
  ["who was marian anderson | tell me about marian anderson", "**Marian Anderson** (1897–1993) was an African American singer with a powerful contralto voice. When she was refused a concert hall because of her race, she sang outdoors at the **Lincoln Memorial** in 1939 for 75,000 people."],
  ["who was dorothea lange | who took the migrant mother photo", "**Dorothea Lange** (1895–1965) was an American photographer famous for photos of struggling families during the Great Depression. Her 1936 photo **Migrant Mother** is one of the most recognized images in American history."],
  ["who was don bradman | who was the best cricketer ever", "**Sir Donald Bradman** (1908–2001) was an Australian cricketer widely regarded as the greatest batsman of all time. His Test batting average of **99.94** is far higher than any other player's."],
  ["who was florence griffith joyner | who was flo jo | tell me about flo jo", "**Florence Griffith Joyner** (1959–1998), known as \"Flo-Jo,\" was an American sprinter who won three gold medals at the 1988 Olympics. Her world records in the 100 m and 200 m, set in 1988, still stand, and she was known for her colorful long nails."],
  ["who was eusebio | tell me about eusebio", "**Eusébio** (1942–2014) was a Mozambique-born Portuguese soccer star nicknamed \"the Black Panther.\" He was the top scorer at the 1966 World Cup and scored over 600 goals for the club Benfica."],
  ["who was sonja henie | tell me about sonja henie", "**Sonja Henie** (1912–1969) was a Norwegian figure skater who won **three Olympic gold medals** in a row (1928, 1932 and 1936) and ten world championships. She later became a Hollywood movie star."],
  ["what is elvis presley famous for | why is elvis called the king", "**Elvis Presley** is called the **\"King of Rock and Roll\"** because he brought rock and roll to a huge audience in the 1950s, mixing blues, country and gospel. His energetic performances, famous hip-shaking dance moves and over 500 million records sold made him a legend."],
  ["what is shakespeare's most famous line | to be or not to be", "One of **Shakespeare's** most famous lines is **\"To be, or not to be, that is the question,\"** spoken by Prince Hamlet in *Hamlet* (around 1600). Other famous lines include \"Romeo, Romeo, wherefore art thou Romeo?\" from *Romeo and Juliet*."]
);
